1
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:02,950
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:03,930
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:05,400
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:13,120
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,480
Hi, everyone.

6
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:15,940
So we're going to get started.

7
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:21,540
I'm the third instructor for this course,

8
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:24,160
so I'll just quickly introduce myself.

9
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,200
I'm Phillip Isola.

10
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,820
I also go by Phil, so feel free to call me by my first name.

11
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:30,720
Just Phil is fine.

12
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,000
And I am a professor in EECS.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,500
Just-- I love deep learning.

14
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,960
Deep learning is, for me, the most beautiful kind

15
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:44,920
of math and intelligence that I could imagine.

16
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:48,500
I think that it's a very popular subject, obviously,

17
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,200
but sometimes deep learning gets a little bit of flack

18
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,252
because maybe it's too hyped or because people feel

19
00:00:56,252 --> 00:00:57,460
like too much money is in it.

20
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,752
So there's always this weird pushback to deep learning.

21
00:00:59,752 --> 00:01:01,400
And I just want to say, I just love it.

22
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:02,002
It's so cool.

23
00:01:02,002 --> 00:01:02,960
And it's OK to love it.

24
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,440
We're going to try to convey why it's so cool.

25
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:08,220
So deep learning to me is just the most--

26
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:10,300
my favorite subject of all, and it's

27
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,220
great to get to teach you about it.

28
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:14,940
OK, so there's a lot of beautiful things

29
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:16,020
about deep learning.

30
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:17,440
You've already heard some of them.

31
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,315
And today we're going to hear one more, which

32
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:21,220
is graph neural networks.

33
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:23,300
This is a continuation of the sequence

34
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,260
that we have going on architectures.

35
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:27,440
So we have four lectures.

36
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:29,820
We have the one that Sarah gave on Tuesday

37
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:31,040
on convolutional networks.

38
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,623
Now we're going to see graph nets that

39
00:01:32,623 --> 00:01:35,682
are a generalization of ConvNets, along with a lot

40
00:01:35,682 --> 00:01:36,640
of other architectures.

41
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,223
And then we'll see transformers, which

42
00:01:38,223 --> 00:01:40,220
are a special kind of graph net and we'll

43
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:42,820
see recurrent networks and memory-based architectures

44
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:45,800
as well over the next few weeks.

45
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:52,100
So the outline of this talk is going to be,

46
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:54,740
first, talking about what types of problems

47
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:57,920
are appropriate to use graph nets on.

48
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:02,700
And then we'll talk about the main way that graph nets work.

49
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,380
We'll present that as a message-passing algorithm,

50
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,960
but you'll see that it turns out to be very familiar.

51
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,759
It's just another neural network that

52
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:14,320
has a forward pass and a backwards pass.

53
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:16,320
And then the last section of this lecture

54
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:17,820
will be on approximation power.

55
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,880
So this will be continuing on from the approximation lecture

56
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:23,600
that Jeremy gave, looking at, what types of functions

57
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:26,120
can graph nets approximate?

58
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,540
To what degree are they universal?

59
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:29,960
What is their capacity to represent

60
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,480
different types of functions?

61
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:34,720
And one of the interesting things

62
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:36,360
I want to point out here at the start

63
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:40,280
is that graph nets are not universal in the same way

64
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,102
that MLPs are.

65
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:44,060
And in fact, that's where the power comes from.

66
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:47,040
And that's what Sarah mentioned at the beginning of last lecture

67
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:51,280
as well, that the idea of architecture design

68
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:54,320
is to make systems that are maybe

69
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:57,528
better to run on hardware, on GPUs, to make systems that

70
00:02:57,528 --> 00:02:59,070
are easy to parallelize and so forth.

71
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:01,290
But another big part of architecture design

72
00:03:01,290 --> 00:03:03,650
is adding constraints.

73
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:06,130
So making a function approximator

74
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:09,690
that actually can't fit certain types of functions because we

75
00:03:09,690 --> 00:03:13,730
want to rule those types of functions out.

76
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:15,930
Graph nets and graphs are a language

77
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:19,930
for introducing constraints that limit your system from doing

78
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:21,352
things you don't want it to do.

79
00:03:21,352 --> 00:03:22,810
And this is one of the perspectives

80
00:03:22,810 --> 00:03:26,290
on architecture design that we'll keep on coming back to.

81
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:27,888
We want to be able to fit the data,

82
00:03:27,888 --> 00:03:29,930
but we want to constrain ourselves to fit it only

83
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:31,130
in the right ways.

84
00:03:31,130 --> 00:03:33,850
So universality is actually not what we're

85
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:37,290
after in architecture design.

86
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:40,150
But within the family of functions that we do care about,

87
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:43,430
we do want the architecture to build approximate them,

88
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:48,370
and we'll see what family graph nets can approximate.

89
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:52,330
Quick reminder on logistics is that problem sets are due

90
00:03:52,330 --> 00:03:53,490
next Tuesday.

91
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:55,870
So hopefully, you're making good progress.

92
00:03:55,870 --> 00:03:58,003
But if you haven't started, start now.

93
00:03:58,003 --> 00:03:59,170
It's a two-week problem set.

94
00:03:59,170 --> 00:04:00,503
There's a lot of stuff in there.

95
00:04:03,470 --> 00:04:06,070
OK, so let's talk about what types of problems

96
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:07,830
graph nets are amenable to.

97
00:04:07,830 --> 00:04:12,030
And maybe before I tell you some examples that I have in mind,

98
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:13,370
I could just ask the audience.

99
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:17,750
So graph nets are going to be appropriate for problems that

100
00:04:17,750 --> 00:04:21,350
naturally can be specified with a graph or data

101
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:23,110
types that exist on a graph.

102
00:04:23,110 --> 00:04:26,750
So in your work and your fields that you're coming from,

103
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:28,310
what are some problems that you can

104
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:32,650
think of that are well-stated as graph problems?

105
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:33,150
OK.

106
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:34,147
Yeah, let's go up here.

107
00:04:34,147 --> 00:04:35,230
AUDIENCE: Social networks?

108
00:04:35,230 --> 00:04:36,980
PHILLIP ISOLA: So one are social networks.

109
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:40,270
Social networks are graphs, people and their friends

110
00:04:40,270 --> 00:04:41,450
or their connections.

111
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:42,203
OK, another one?

112
00:04:42,203 --> 00:04:44,370
AUDIENCE: Hidden Markov models or time-variant data?

113
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:46,078
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, hidden Markov models

114
00:04:46,078 --> 00:04:48,690
are one kind of probabilistic graphical model,

115
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:51,430
and this idea of graphical models for representing

116
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:54,520
probability distributions is a classical idea,

117
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:56,770
and graph nets are appropriate for representing things

118
00:04:56,770 --> 00:04:57,530
like this.

119
00:04:57,530 --> 00:04:58,730
OK, back here.

120
00:04:58,730 --> 00:05:00,170
AUDIENCE: Gene interactions.

121
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:01,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: Gene interactions.

122
00:05:01,270 --> 00:05:01,770
Yes.

123
00:05:01,770 --> 00:05:03,530
I'll have some examples on that.

124
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:06,810
So whenever I have different genes that

125
00:05:06,810 --> 00:05:11,450
are interacting with each other or with proteins that

126
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:14,050
get transcribed from those genes,

127
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:16,930
then these could be thought of as nodes and edges that

128
00:05:16,930 --> 00:05:18,030
represent relationships.

129
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:19,627
Let's do just one more.

130
00:05:19,627 --> 00:05:21,710
AUDIENCE: Cross referencing of different websites.

131
00:05:21,710 --> 00:05:23,877
PHILLIP ISOLA: Cross referencing different websites.

132
00:05:23,877 --> 00:05:26,930
So things like PageRank, hyperlinks between different web

133
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:27,430
pages.

134
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:30,550
Like, Google started as a graph search company, essentially.

135
00:05:30,550 --> 00:05:32,590
They represent the web as a graph of hyperlinks,

136
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:36,370
and then they try to evaluate where the important nodes are

137
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:37,810
in that graph.

138
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:42,570
So whatever problem you have, if you

139
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:45,590
have studied physics or chemistry or computer science,

140
00:05:45,590 --> 00:05:47,505
I'm sure that you have seen a million graphs.

141
00:05:47,505 --> 00:05:49,130
That's one of the basic data structures

142
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:53,350
we use for representing the interesting types of data

143
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:55,510
in the world.

144
00:05:55,510 --> 00:05:59,190
So graph nets are an appropriate technology for problems

145
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:00,610
that naturally exist on graphs.

146
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:02,027
And there's a few different things

147
00:06:02,027 --> 00:06:04,330
we might want to ask about a graph.

148
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:07,910
So one thing is you might want to take a node in a graph.

149
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:09,530
Maybe this is a social network graph.

150
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:11,380
This is a user in that social network.

151
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,130
You want to classify something about them.

152
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:17,430
You want to say, is this person somebody

153
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,210
who likes jazz music or not?

154
00:06:20,210 --> 00:06:22,190
Maybe that's the prediction problem

155
00:06:22,190 --> 00:06:25,343
that your social media company wants to make.

156
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:27,010
So that's a node classification problem.

157
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:29,797
We can ask a question about a node.

158
00:06:29,797 --> 00:06:31,630
Other times you might want to ask a question

159
00:06:31,630 --> 00:06:32,690
about the entire graph.

160
00:06:32,690 --> 00:06:35,830
What is the average number of friends that people have

161
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:37,010
within this social network?

162
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:38,450
Maybe that's a property that I care about.

163
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:39,950
So we can ask questions about nodes,

164
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,673
about edges, relationships, about the entire graph.

165
00:06:42,673 --> 00:06:44,590
I'm going to give you a few different examples

166
00:06:44,590 --> 00:06:48,510
just to motivate why graphs are so important.

167
00:06:48,510 --> 00:06:50,710
So another one might be this--

168
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:53,600
I think this is from Pinterest.

169
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:57,540
So it's users and the boards that they're interested in.

170
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:00,500
So these topic areas that they're interested in.

171
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,600
So assigning you to, what are the topics

172
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:06,320
that you're interested in on this website that

173
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:08,920
allows you to see content under these topics?

174
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:12,100
So this is a graph between people and their interest.

175
00:07:14,970 --> 00:07:17,620
These types of graphs are often used in recommender systems.

176
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,703
I want to recommend what you might be interested--

177
00:07:19,703 --> 00:07:22,160
what next video on YouTube would you want to watch?

178
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:24,000
So it's like a bipartite graph between users

179
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,080
and their interests or users and content.

180
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:33,080
So I think we had the gene expression idea previously.

181
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:37,400
But in general, for biology and especially chemistry,

182
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:41,660
molecular biology, we have graphs which are molecules.

183
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:44,420
Molecules are well-represented as graphs.

184
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:47,040
There are atoms and bonds between the atoms.

185
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:51,500
And if we want to do things like predict which--

186
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:55,300
where would the binding site be on that molecule for some drug,

187
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:58,960
or would this molecule be toxic to a human,

188
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:00,460
these are graph prediction problems.

189
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,180
I'm trying to ask a question about that graph, which

190
00:08:03,180 --> 00:08:04,560
is the molecule structure.

191
00:08:08,075 --> 00:08:13,140
So in biology and chemistry, graph nets are used extensively.

192
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:17,900
Here's another example where we have the graph of interactions

193
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:22,300
between different drugs and the drug's effect on the human

194
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:24,020
for some kind of medication.

195
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:26,840
So we might have, in this example,

196
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:31,340
two different drugs that can have a positive effect on you.

197
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:33,260
But if you combine them, they're negative.

198
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:36,283
Interactions between drugs that can be bad.

199
00:08:36,283 --> 00:08:37,700
You always have to read the bottle

200
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:39,037
on the pills you're taking.

201
00:08:39,037 --> 00:08:41,120
Like, don't take this if you have this other drug.

202
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:45,560
So that can be represented as a more complicated graph.

203
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:48,180
Now, in this graph, we have edges

204
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:50,797
between pharmaceutical interventions,

205
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:52,880
like the drugs that you're going to take, and also

206
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,500
edges between the drugs and the proteins that they affect.

207
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:58,000
And so different types of edges and different types of nodes

208
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,040
can exist in a graph, and we are going

209
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:03,400
to be wanting to learn properties

210
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,120
of these edges and these nodes so that we can make predictions

211
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:08,820
about what combination of drugs might be toxic.

212
00:09:12,996 --> 00:09:16,480
So graphs also come up in just classical computer science

213
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:17,300
algorithms.

214
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:21,600
So in shortest path algorithms, in graph search

215
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,700
of various forms, tree search algorithms,

216
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:28,840
we often represent classical, discrete optimization problems

217
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:31,540
as some kind of search of a graph.

218
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:32,620
So here's one example.

219
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:35,160
This is from Google Maps.

220
00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:39,080
And they're trying to find the shortest path, the fastest

221
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,920
driving route between your house and where you want to go.

222
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:45,385
So you probably use Google Maps to do this all the time.

223
00:09:45,385 --> 00:09:46,260
So how do they do it?

224
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:48,720
Well, this is a huge, huge graph.

225
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:50,620
It's a whole world of roads.

226
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:53,340
So it's slow to use classical algorithms

227
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:55,317
like Dijkstra's algorithm.

228
00:09:55,317 --> 00:09:56,900
And additionally, there might be a lot

229
00:09:56,900 --> 00:10:01,900
of constraints beyond just searching over the edge

230
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:03,900
lengths of that road network.

231
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:07,400
It might try to optimize for other properties,

232
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:10,892
like how safe or scenic is the road, or avoid highways,

233
00:10:10,892 --> 00:10:13,600
or there's different constraints that you can add on top of this.

234
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:15,980
So it becomes a very difficult graph search problem

235
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:17,360
to solve with classical methods.

236
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:19,780
It might be too slow.

237
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:21,900
And so one use of graph neural networks

238
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:25,100
is to find heuristics and approximate graph search

239
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:27,880
algorithms that can solve these problems more quickly.

240
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:33,700
So Google, in this example, is using a graph neural network

241
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:37,740
to do faster search than classical algorithms,

242
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:41,260
and we'll talk a little bit more about that as well,

243
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:43,310
by learning heuristics

244
00:10:43,310 --> 00:10:45,560
Another one-- I don't think anybody mentioned physics,

245
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:48,970
but that's another place where graphs come up all the time.

246
00:10:48,970 --> 00:10:53,590
So one of the popular ways to simulate fluids or particles

247
00:10:53,590 --> 00:10:57,870
or atoms is by thinking of them as a set of nodes

248
00:10:57,870 --> 00:11:00,230
with edges between the nodes that are

249
00:11:00,230 --> 00:11:02,430
indicating the physical forces.

250
00:11:02,430 --> 00:11:04,250
So two particles might have an edge,

251
00:11:04,250 --> 00:11:06,910
and that edge is describing the repelling force

252
00:11:06,910 --> 00:11:09,310
between those two particles because we

253
00:11:09,310 --> 00:11:11,770
know that two atoms can't occupy the same space,

254
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:14,590
and they might have electrical forces that push them apart.

255
00:11:14,590 --> 00:11:17,470
So in this example, it's a particle system that

256
00:11:17,470 --> 00:11:19,950
simulates fluid dynamics, and this

257
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:22,110
is represented as a graph neural net that

258
00:11:22,110 --> 00:11:25,530
takes in the graph of all of these water particles,

259
00:11:25,530 --> 00:11:27,750
and the edges between them are going

260
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:30,610
to learn to push apart those nodes, essentially.

261
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:35,397
So just more examples, and there's

262
00:11:35,397 --> 00:11:37,230
some paper references if you want to read up

263
00:11:37,230 --> 00:11:40,350
on these in more detail.

264
00:11:40,350 --> 00:11:46,030
And then most abstractly, just a lot of optimization problems,

265
00:11:46,030 --> 00:11:47,510
especially discrete optimization,

266
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:52,370
combinatorial optimization, take the form of a graph problem

267
00:11:52,370 --> 00:11:56,908
where we might have a set of decision variables

268
00:11:56,908 --> 00:11:57,950
we're trying to optimize.

269
00:11:57,950 --> 00:12:01,090
We're trying to find, what is the setting of these xs that

270
00:12:01,090 --> 00:12:04,030
will satisfy a system of constraints?

271
00:12:04,030 --> 00:12:07,090
So on the right-hand side here, we have a linear program.

272
00:12:07,090 --> 00:12:09,650
We're trying to minimize this linear cost function

273
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:11,290
subject to some constraints.

274
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:15,370
And that can be set up as a graph of constraints that

275
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:18,330
will penalize a certain kind of interaction between the decision

276
00:12:18,330 --> 00:12:19,195
variables, x.

277
00:12:19,195 --> 00:12:21,570
And we want to find the setting of the decision variables

278
00:12:21,570 --> 00:12:23,370
that satisfy those constraints.

279
00:12:23,370 --> 00:12:26,072
And so we can phrase this in the language of graphs,

280
00:12:26,072 --> 00:12:27,530
and there are graph neural networks

281
00:12:27,530 --> 00:12:29,850
that can help us solve these types of problems

282
00:12:29,850 --> 00:12:30,947
more efficiently.

283
00:12:30,947 --> 00:12:32,530
And something I want to point out here

284
00:12:32,530 --> 00:12:35,610
is that, again, just like in the shortest path example

285
00:12:35,610 --> 00:12:38,290
I gave, we do, often for these problems,

286
00:12:38,290 --> 00:12:43,410
have classical algorithms that we know will find the solution.

287
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:45,190
And so one question you might have in mind

288
00:12:45,190 --> 00:12:47,130
is, why would we bother with a learning algorithm?

289
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:49,110
Why would we bother with a neural network that's

290
00:12:49,110 --> 00:12:50,652
going to do something that we already

291
00:12:50,652 --> 00:12:52,890
have the optimal classical algorithm for?

292
00:12:52,890 --> 00:12:56,753
We know exactly how to solve a linear program on the right.

293
00:12:56,753 --> 00:12:58,670
We know exactly how to solve for shortest path

294
00:12:58,670 --> 00:13:01,190
in a graph on the left.

295
00:13:01,190 --> 00:13:05,430
And the reason is, essentially, that these classical algorithms

296
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:07,030
can be slow.

297
00:13:07,030 --> 00:13:11,570
And if we want to use a learning-based system,

298
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:13,790
we can potentially learn a faster way

299
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:16,470
of finding an approximate solution.

300
00:13:16,470 --> 00:13:18,830
And the trade-off is that the classical algorithm

301
00:13:18,830 --> 00:13:20,750
will find-- will be guaranteed to converge

302
00:13:20,750 --> 00:13:24,430
to the true solution.

303
00:13:24,430 --> 00:13:25,510
But it might be slow.

304
00:13:25,510 --> 00:13:27,370
And the neural net, the graph net,

305
00:13:27,370 --> 00:13:30,430
might be able to use heuristics that

306
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:33,430
work really well on the data distribution we train it on.

307
00:13:33,430 --> 00:13:36,150
And so it will solve the problem more quickly on the data

308
00:13:36,150 --> 00:13:38,610
distribution that we're training on,

309
00:13:38,610 --> 00:13:40,870
but it won't be guaranteed to find the right answer

310
00:13:40,870 --> 00:13:42,650
on other data distributions.

311
00:13:42,650 --> 00:13:44,090
So you're learning something that

312
00:13:44,090 --> 00:13:46,770
is more specialized to that data distribution.

313
00:13:46,770 --> 00:13:48,030
OK, cool.

314
00:13:50,730 --> 00:13:55,490
So now let's get into the technical content of how

315
00:13:55,490 --> 00:13:59,050
these graph nets work, how do they represent data.

316
00:13:59,050 --> 00:14:02,950
And this is how we'll represent graphs for this lecture.

317
00:14:02,950 --> 00:14:05,950
We'll say that a graph is a set of nodes.

318
00:14:05,950 --> 00:14:09,790
Every node is going to come with a attribute vector.

319
00:14:09,790 --> 00:14:15,730
So this is going to be our input to our graph processing

320
00:14:15,730 --> 00:14:16,890
algorithm.

321
00:14:16,890 --> 00:14:19,650
We will have a attribute vector for each node.

322
00:14:19,650 --> 00:14:21,770
If these are molecules, that attribute vector

323
00:14:21,770 --> 00:14:23,350
might tell us what is the atom.

324
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:24,750
Is this carbon?

325
00:14:24,750 --> 00:14:26,090
Is this hydrogen?

326
00:14:26,090 --> 00:14:28,810
So in gray, we have this vector, which

327
00:14:28,810 --> 00:14:31,130
is the initial data, our observation

328
00:14:31,130 --> 00:14:34,310
about that node in the graph.

329
00:14:34,310 --> 00:14:36,690
And what we're going to try to learn

330
00:14:36,690 --> 00:14:40,610
is a mapping from our graph nodes

331
00:14:40,610 --> 00:14:43,422
to representations of the nodes that

332
00:14:43,422 --> 00:14:45,630
are going to be more informative about the properties

333
00:14:45,630 --> 00:14:48,350
that we care to predict.

334
00:14:48,350 --> 00:14:50,830
Just like we can do take an image patch

335
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:53,803
and embed it into a vector, we can also take a node in a graph

336
00:14:53,803 --> 00:14:55,470
and embed it into a vector that tells us

337
00:14:55,470 --> 00:14:57,430
something important about that node,

338
00:14:57,430 --> 00:15:03,350
maybe classifies is it a binding site for the drug of interest?

339
00:15:03,350 --> 00:15:07,110
Additionally, we will represent the edges between the graph

340
00:15:07,110 --> 00:15:09,310
with an adjacency matrix, and this

341
00:15:09,310 --> 00:15:11,810
will be an additional input to our graph neural network.

342
00:15:11,810 --> 00:15:13,990
So we'll have a set of nodes which

343
00:15:13,990 --> 00:15:16,730
are represented by vectors of observed data

344
00:15:16,730 --> 00:15:17,853
and an adjacency matrix.

345
00:15:17,853 --> 00:15:19,270
And then we'll do some computation

346
00:15:19,270 --> 00:15:22,270
on that to make a prediction about the nodes.

347
00:15:22,270 --> 00:15:25,093
We can also make a prediction about the entire graph itself,

348
00:15:25,093 --> 00:15:27,010
and that's sometimes called a graph embedding.

349
00:15:27,010 --> 00:15:29,350
So this is graph to vec right.

350
00:15:29,350 --> 00:15:30,970
On the left side, we have node to vec.

351
00:15:30,970 --> 00:15:33,470
Every node gets embedded into some feature

352
00:15:33,470 --> 00:15:36,090
vector that tells us something of interest about it.

353
00:15:36,090 --> 00:15:38,470
On the right, we're going to just embed the entire graph

354
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:41,160
into a feature vector that tells something of interest

355
00:15:41,160 --> 00:15:42,520
about the entire graph.

356
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:45,620
For example, is this molecule toxic or not?

357
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:47,680
So we can ask questions about nodes in a graph

358
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:49,380
or about the entire graph itself.

359
00:15:53,370 --> 00:15:57,400
So GNNs-- graph neural networks are GNNs.

360
00:15:57,400 --> 00:16:02,280
They learn a function from these adjacency matrices that tell us

361
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:05,200
the edge structure and the set of graph node

362
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:09,500
attributes that are observed to a set of embedding vectors

363
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:12,900
or predictions about the nodes or the entire graph itself.

364
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:17,610
Do I see a question in the--

365
00:16:17,610 --> 00:16:18,860
I think I see a question here.

366
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:19,660
Yeah.

367
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:22,520
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

368
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:24,240
Graph embeddings.

369
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:26,600
Do they do the same model?

370
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:27,887
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

371
00:16:27,887 --> 00:16:29,720
So the question is, what is the relationship

372
00:16:29,720 --> 00:16:31,740
between node embeddings and graph embeddings.

373
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:33,480
And we'll see more details on this.

374
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:38,360
But the node embedding is going to be a vector that we learn

375
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:40,460
for each node in a graph, and the graph embedding

376
00:16:40,460 --> 00:16:42,960
is going to be one vector for the entire graph.

377
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:44,700
We'll see examples of this.

378
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:46,600
And then I saw one other question.

379
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:47,180
Good?

380
00:16:47,180 --> 00:16:48,140
OK.

381
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:49,280
In the back?

382
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,100
AUDIENCE: For tasks, are you learning

383
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:53,320
either the node embedding or the graph embedding,

384
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:56,060
or do you typically join [INAUDIBLE]?

385
00:16:56,060 --> 00:16:58,823
PHILLIP ISOLA: So are we going to learn either node embedding

386
00:16:58,823 --> 00:17:01,240
or graph embedding, or are we going to jointly learn them?

387
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:02,240
You could do either one.

388
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:03,040
You could do both.

389
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:08,940
Well, in general, we might learn a set of node embeddings

390
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:11,839
and then sum them up at the end to get a graph embedding.

391
00:17:11,839 --> 00:17:13,863
That would be one way of combining the two.

392
00:17:13,863 --> 00:17:14,780
OK, one more question.

393
00:17:14,780 --> 00:17:15,859
Then I'll move on.

394
00:17:15,859 --> 00:17:18,845
Yeah, over here.

395
00:17:18,845 --> 00:17:19,720
Sorry, in the middle.

396
00:17:19,720 --> 00:17:22,140
Yeah.

397
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:24,099
AUDIENCE: How much do we care about the size

398
00:17:24,099 --> 00:17:26,940
of the adjacency matrix and [INAUDIBLE] number

399
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:29,460
of nodes [INAUDIBLE] dimensionality

400
00:17:29,460 --> 00:17:31,980
of the [INAUDIBLE] those are constrained

401
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:35,715
not at all [INAUDIBLE]

402
00:17:35,715 --> 00:17:37,340
PHILLIP ISOLA: I think, if I understood

403
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:41,760
the question- how much do we care about the dimensionality

404
00:17:41,760 --> 00:17:44,520
of the adjacency matrix?

405
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:47,180
If we have a really big graph with a lot of connections,

406
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:48,420
does this become intractable?

407
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:51,720
And I would say that's more-- that's not really

408
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:53,180
our choice as modelers.

409
00:17:53,180 --> 00:17:55,780
That's going to be the property of the data

410
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:56,780
that we're working with.

411
00:17:56,780 --> 00:17:59,720
So yes, if we have a graph-- if our data type that we're working

412
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:01,860
with are molecules are just very densely connected,

413
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:03,902
then this will be something we have to deal with,

414
00:18:03,902 --> 00:18:07,400
and that will be computationally more expensive to deal with.

415
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:11,487
But that's-- we don't usually try to simplify the input graph.

416
00:18:11,487 --> 00:18:13,320
We're going to take the input graph as given

417
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:15,320
and ask a question about it.

418
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:17,920
Yeah.

419
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:23,240
So before we get to how graph nets work, let's

420
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:25,960
first ask, what if we just wanted

421
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:29,160
to make a prediction about graph-structured data

422
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:30,060
with an MLP?

423
00:18:30,060 --> 00:18:31,960
We've already seen MLPs.

424
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:37,340
So MLP's another name for that fully connected neural network.

425
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:40,420
What if we just use this fully connected neural network?

426
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:44,220
How would we take as input the properties of the graph?

427
00:18:44,220 --> 00:18:45,860
So one thing that we need as input

428
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:47,620
is the adjacency matrix that tells

429
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:51,300
us which nodes in the graph are connected to which nodes.

430
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:56,200
And just to quickly show you what that looks like--

431
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:56,700
let's see.

432
00:18:56,700 --> 00:18:59,060
What happened to my mouse?

433
00:18:59,060 --> 00:19:00,340
How do I find my mouse?

434
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:01,140
OK.

435
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:04,060
Well, anyway, you can see the adjacency matrix up here

436
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:07,980
on the left, and this is just telling us

437
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:10,233
which nodes in the graph up at the top right

438
00:19:10,233 --> 00:19:11,400
are connected to each other.

439
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:14,940
So 1 means there's an edge between the node index i

440
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:16,840
and the node index j in that matrix.

441
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:19,100
And a 0 means there's no edge.

442
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:22,880
So what is the fourth row of that adjacency matrix?

443
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:25,920
Which node in the graph at the top right does that refer to?

444
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:29,540
Blue?

445
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:30,200
OK, I heard it.

446
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:30,340
Yeah.

447
00:19:30,340 --> 00:19:31,040
So blue, right?

448
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:35,150
So there's three 1s, and that's the only node in that graph that

449
00:19:35,150 --> 00:19:37,350
has three edges, so that's how you would know that

450
00:19:37,350 --> 00:19:39,230
that's the relationship.

451
00:19:39,230 --> 00:19:43,750
So the adjacency matrix tells us the connectivity of the graph.

452
00:19:43,750 --> 00:19:46,710
And we additionally would have a set of attribute vectors

453
00:19:46,710 --> 00:19:50,590
for every single node, the observed vector for that node.

454
00:19:50,590 --> 00:19:54,110
And we could just put these into an MLP

455
00:19:54,110 --> 00:19:58,630
by simply creating a serialization

456
00:19:58,630 --> 00:20:01,670
of our adjacency matrix and all the node

457
00:20:01,670 --> 00:20:03,850
attribute vectors and just one really big vector

458
00:20:03,850 --> 00:20:05,970
and just put that into a multi-layer perceptron.

459
00:20:05,970 --> 00:20:12,330
So can anyone think of why that might not be the best strategy?

460
00:20:12,330 --> 00:20:14,870
What might be an undesirable property

461
00:20:14,870 --> 00:20:18,070
of just serializing our adjacency matrix

462
00:20:18,070 --> 00:20:20,670
and our node attributes?

463
00:20:20,670 --> 00:20:22,190
Let's go over here.

464
00:20:22,190 --> 00:20:25,030
AUDIENCE: So adjacency matrices can only consist of 1s and 0s,

465
00:20:25,030 --> 00:20:27,750
whereas MLPs can take in real numbers.

466
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:28,750
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK, yeah.

467
00:20:28,750 --> 00:20:31,435
So adjacency matrices only consist of 1s and 0s.

468
00:20:31,435 --> 00:20:33,810
Maybe we're not making use of the full capacity of an MLP

469
00:20:33,810 --> 00:20:34,950
to take in real numbers.

470
00:20:34,950 --> 00:20:37,030
That's a good idea.

471
00:20:37,030 --> 00:20:39,750
AUDIENCE: It depends on the index you put on the nodes.

472
00:20:39,750 --> 00:20:44,010
So if you permute, it's not going to work.

473
00:20:44,010 --> 00:20:44,930
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

474
00:20:44,930 --> 00:20:47,810
So the answer-- and this is the answer that I always had in

475
00:20:47,810 --> 00:20:54,530
mind myself, is the adjacency matrix, represented as a vector,

476
00:20:54,530 --> 00:20:59,330
is going to depend on how we assigned indices to the nodes

477
00:20:59,330 --> 00:21:00,330
in the graph.

478
00:21:00,330 --> 00:21:03,410
And that decision was arbitrary.

479
00:21:03,410 --> 00:21:06,270
We decided that the fourth row of the adjacency matrix--

480
00:21:06,270 --> 00:21:08,110
the index 4 is the blue node.

481
00:21:08,110 --> 00:21:11,410
But why didn't I say that index 3 is the blue node.

482
00:21:11,410 --> 00:21:14,590
So generally, for graph-structured data,

483
00:21:14,590 --> 00:21:19,290
we want to be processing it in a way that is invariant to how we

484
00:21:19,290 --> 00:21:21,622
chose to index into the nodes.

485
00:21:21,622 --> 00:21:23,330
The structure of the graph, the structure

486
00:21:23,330 --> 00:21:26,730
of a model-- of a molecule in many types of graph data

487
00:21:26,730 --> 00:21:31,570
don't really depend on the ordering of the nodes.

488
00:21:31,570 --> 00:21:33,390
So in math terms, we would say that we

489
00:21:33,390 --> 00:21:36,750
want a function which is permutation-invariant

490
00:21:36,750 --> 00:21:41,710
to permuting the indices of the adjacency matrix

491
00:21:41,710 --> 00:21:45,310
and permuting the ordering of the node attribute

492
00:21:45,310 --> 00:21:47,910
vectors that we put into our system.

493
00:21:47,910 --> 00:21:52,510
So here is a permutation matrix, P,

494
00:21:52,510 --> 00:21:56,430
applied to the rows and the columns of our adjacency matrix.

495
00:21:56,430 --> 00:22:02,230
And the graph that is described by this permutation

496
00:22:02,230 --> 00:22:04,150
is the same, structurally, as the graph

497
00:22:04,150 --> 00:22:07,290
that's described by the original adjacency matrix.

498
00:22:07,290 --> 00:22:10,910
We've just chosen a different ordering of the indexes

499
00:22:10,910 --> 00:22:12,270
to label the nodes.

500
00:22:12,270 --> 00:22:16,110
So the first matrix, P, permutes the rows,

501
00:22:16,110 --> 00:22:19,990
and the P transpose permute the columns.

502
00:22:19,990 --> 00:22:24,490
Additionally, if we have a matrix of node attributes--

503
00:22:24,490 --> 00:22:28,990
so we have four nodes, and so we'll have a 4-by-D-dimensional

504
00:22:28,990 --> 00:22:31,010
matrix, X, of the node attributes.

505
00:22:31,010 --> 00:22:34,730
This is the observed data at each node.

506
00:22:34,730 --> 00:22:38,930
We can permute that with our permutation matrix, P.

507
00:22:38,930 --> 00:22:42,530
And in order for the graph net, f, or the function,

508
00:22:42,530 --> 00:22:45,810
f, to be invariant to these types of reindexing

509
00:22:45,810 --> 00:22:49,810
of our graph, we would want that permuting the adjacency matrix

510
00:22:49,810 --> 00:22:52,370
and permuting the nodes-- the node attributes

511
00:22:52,370 --> 00:22:57,290
will result in the same answer as any other permutation.

512
00:22:57,290 --> 00:23:00,170
So this property's called permutation invariance.

513
00:23:00,170 --> 00:23:03,850
Very related to what we heard about on Tuesday.

514
00:23:03,850 --> 00:23:07,810
Where convolutional networks are invariant or equivariant

515
00:23:07,810 --> 00:23:10,210
to translation, graph nets are going

516
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:16,930
to be invariant or equivariant to permutations of the inputs.

517
00:23:16,930 --> 00:23:20,550
So permutation equivariance is almost the same thing.

518
00:23:20,550 --> 00:23:24,090
It just says that if you permute the way that you

519
00:23:24,090 --> 00:23:28,350
have indexed into the data, then you should permute your outputs.

520
00:23:28,350 --> 00:23:32,790
So if I shuffle my nodes, then I should end up

521
00:23:32,790 --> 00:23:35,670
shuffling my predictions.

522
00:23:35,670 --> 00:23:38,610
So same idea as translation invariance versus equivariance.

523
00:23:38,610 --> 00:23:41,470
I would want to be permutation-invariant

524
00:23:41,470 --> 00:23:44,910
if I'm trying to, let's say, predict something

525
00:23:44,910 --> 00:23:47,070
about the entire graph.

526
00:23:47,070 --> 00:23:49,410
So no matter how I index those nodes,

527
00:23:49,410 --> 00:23:52,050
then the prediction about the graph will be the same.

528
00:23:52,050 --> 00:23:54,710
And if I want to predict something about every node,

529
00:23:54,710 --> 00:23:56,330
like I want to output node embeddings,

530
00:23:56,330 --> 00:23:58,870
then I would want to be permutation-equivariant so

531
00:23:58,870 --> 00:24:01,510
that if I shuffle the nodes, my predictions about the nodes

532
00:24:01,510 --> 00:24:04,230
get shuffled in the same way, just

533
00:24:04,230 --> 00:24:06,790
like I want translation equivariance if I want

534
00:24:06,790 --> 00:24:10,710
to have the output of my convolutional network

535
00:24:10,710 --> 00:24:13,790
have the property that if I shuffle or translate the input

536
00:24:13,790 --> 00:24:17,710
image, I will translate the dense set of predictions

537
00:24:17,710 --> 00:24:18,650
in the output.

538
00:24:18,650 --> 00:24:20,890
I'll translate the feature map that comes out.

539
00:24:23,510 --> 00:24:25,430
So this is the kind of fundamental symmetry

540
00:24:25,430 --> 00:24:28,720
that we're going to be exploiting with graph neural

541
00:24:28,720 --> 00:24:29,560
networks.

542
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:33,480
They'll be appropriate for processing data for which

543
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:35,160
the problem of interest is actually

544
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:38,640
permutation-equivariant or invariant.

545
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:40,863
Yeah, question.

546
00:24:40,863 --> 00:24:42,280
AUDIENCE: I think I understand why

547
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:46,280
we need to make it invariant, but is that under the assumption

548
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:51,840
that all graphs could be made into this invariant form?

549
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:56,000
What are some problems where the nodes are just not

550
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,680
able to become invariant?

551
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:00,520
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, we'll see, at the end

552
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:02,312
of the class, some cases where you wouldn't

553
00:25:02,312 --> 00:25:05,040
want permutation invariance.

554
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:11,240
But if, for example, you live in a world where molecules always

555
00:25:11,240 --> 00:25:14,440
come in some orientation, like the heavier--

556
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:17,240
the blue is a heavier atom and it always

557
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:20,502
lives on-- it has a position, a canonical position, that you're

558
00:25:20,502 --> 00:25:21,960
always going to observe, then you'd

559
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:23,460
want to use that information.

560
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:24,700
But in our world--

561
00:25:29,060 --> 00:25:31,230
yeah, .

562
00:25:31,230 --> 00:25:32,980
Yeah, I'll have to think a little bit more

563
00:25:32,980 --> 00:25:34,980
about a good example, but we're going to see one

564
00:25:34,980 --> 00:25:37,890
at the end of the class.

565
00:25:37,890 --> 00:25:38,640
One more question.

566
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:39,140
Yeah?

567
00:25:39,140 --> 00:25:40,820
