575
00:25:56,972 --> 00:25:58,900
It just means that if I permute the inputs,

576
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:01,700
then I get the same output no matter what.

577
00:26:01,700 --> 00:26:03,740
And that's going to be most appropriate if I'm

578
00:26:03,740 --> 00:26:06,760
making some prediction about the entire graph.

579
00:26:06,760 --> 00:26:09,240
If I'm making predictions about a single node in the graph,

580
00:26:09,240 --> 00:26:13,940
I want my prediction for the nth node in the graph

581
00:26:13,940 --> 00:26:19,160
to always be in the nth output row of my output matrix

582
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:24,300
if I'm predicting a matrix as output or a set of vectors--

583
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:26,100
vector embeddings per node as output.

584
00:26:28,780 --> 00:26:31,920
So let's, again, make the connection to graph neural

585
00:26:31,920 --> 00:26:34,460
networks-- sorry, to convolutional neural networks.

586
00:26:34,460 --> 00:26:39,000
So ConvNets are graph nets applied on a grid.

587
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:40,760
We said that ConvNets are appropriate

588
00:26:40,760 --> 00:26:42,880
for grid-structured data.

589
00:26:42,880 --> 00:26:45,680
And if we're processing an image, which

590
00:26:45,680 --> 00:26:48,480
is one of the main applications of convolutional networks,

591
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:52,640
then we'll have a grid of pixels or patches.

592
00:26:52,640 --> 00:26:55,220
And we can think of that as just a graph of nodes,

593
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:58,640
and the edges between the nodes are the weights

594
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:00,660
of the convolutional filter.

595
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:05,540
And in a graph net, it's just the same idea,

596
00:27:05,540 --> 00:27:08,160
except generalized to non-grid topologies.

597
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:10,720
So if I want to process something

598
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:12,940
about the node in the center of that green region,

599
00:27:12,940 --> 00:27:14,520
I'm going to look at all my neighbors

600
00:27:14,520 --> 00:27:17,142
and aggregate information from all of them,

601
00:27:17,142 --> 00:27:18,600
just like in a ConvNet, we're going

602
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:21,183
to run a filter that's going to look at all my local neighbors

603
00:27:21,183 --> 00:27:25,540
in a patch around a pixel, or around a node on a grid graph,

604
00:27:25,540 --> 00:27:28,220
and aggregate information to make a new representation

605
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:32,240
of that location in the image.

606
00:27:35,020 --> 00:27:36,040
So one question for you.

607
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:38,760
So graph nets look a lot like ConvNets,

608
00:27:38,760 --> 00:27:40,340
but now they're on arbitrary graphs,

609
00:27:40,340 --> 00:27:42,862
as opposed to just on a grid.

610
00:27:42,862 --> 00:27:44,820
So what are some of the interesting differences

611
00:27:44,820 --> 00:27:47,020
between a ConvNet and a graph net?

612
00:27:49,740 --> 00:27:52,220
What is the new problem you might encounter with a graph

613
00:27:52,220 --> 00:27:53,887
that you won't encounter with a ConvNet?

614
00:27:53,887 --> 00:27:54,460
Yeah.

615
00:27:54,460 --> 00:27:56,043
AUDIENCE: The neighborhood of a vertex

616
00:27:56,043 --> 00:27:57,987
is not the same shape all the time?

617
00:27:57,987 --> 00:27:58,820
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

618
00:27:58,820 --> 00:28:01,058
So the neighborhood of a vertex is not the same shape

619
00:28:01,058 --> 00:28:01,600
all the time.

620
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:05,060
So you need a architecture that can handle having

621
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:06,808
variable numbers of neighbors.

622
00:28:06,808 --> 00:28:09,100
It's not going to be something that's always, like, a 3

623
00:28:09,100 --> 00:28:10,160
by 3 patch.

624
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:12,452
And this is going to have hardware implications, right?

625
00:28:12,452 --> 00:28:15,940
It's going to mean that I can't just have baked onto my hardware

626
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:19,100
that I'm going to look at, a 3 by 3 grid.

627
00:28:19,100 --> 00:28:23,190
I'm going to have to make that flexible enough that if the node

628
00:28:23,190 --> 00:28:25,230
has a lot of edge neighbors, then

629
00:28:25,230 --> 00:28:28,590
I will be able to aggregate over a bigger set.

630
00:28:28,590 --> 00:28:29,130
Any others?

631
00:28:29,130 --> 00:28:29,942
Yeah.

632
00:28:29,942 --> 00:28:32,150
AUDIENCE: There is no structure in the neighborhoods.

633
00:28:32,150 --> 00:28:33,730
All these should be treated equally.

634
00:28:33,730 --> 00:28:34,890
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes, excellent.

635
00:28:34,890 --> 00:28:36,050
There's no structure in the neighborhood.

636
00:28:36,050 --> 00:28:37,300
All should be treated equally.

637
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:41,310
So in a convolutional network, there

638
00:28:41,310 --> 00:28:44,090
is this kind of spatial dimensionality to it,

639
00:28:44,090 --> 00:28:46,550
where left and right have a different meaning than up

640
00:28:46,550 --> 00:28:48,510
and down, and I might want to treat left and right differently

641
00:28:48,510 --> 00:28:49,550
than up and down.

642
00:28:49,550 --> 00:28:52,670
And in a graph net, we actually are

643
00:28:52,670 --> 00:28:57,550
going to represent the operation that

644
00:28:57,550 --> 00:29:00,750
updates the node representation given the neighbors

645
00:29:00,750 --> 00:29:03,750
in a way that is--

646
00:29:03,750 --> 00:29:07,590
doesn't depend on, spatially, where those nodes land.

647
00:29:07,590 --> 00:29:09,070
So the structures are more abstract

648
00:29:09,070 --> 00:29:11,790
than the spatial structure of a ConvNet.

649
00:29:11,790 --> 00:29:14,863
So there are a few important differences,

650
00:29:14,863 --> 00:29:16,530
but there's also a lot of commonalities.

651
00:29:16,530 --> 00:29:18,510
So here's a few of the commonalities.

652
00:29:18,510 --> 00:29:20,670
One is that both of these architectures

653
00:29:20,670 --> 00:29:22,770
are based on local operations.

654
00:29:22,770 --> 00:29:25,490
So we run local convolutional filters

655
00:29:25,490 --> 00:29:28,630
to update our representation of the data at that location.

656
00:29:28,630 --> 00:29:33,170
And we graph local computations where every node in the graph

657
00:29:33,170 --> 00:29:36,507
is going to get input from just its neighbors.

658
00:29:36,507 --> 00:29:38,090
And the way you make a global decision

659
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:40,257
or you propagate information across the entire graph

660
00:29:40,257 --> 00:29:43,650
or the entire image is just by stacking those local operations.

661
00:29:43,650 --> 00:29:46,772
So you can filter an image over and over again,

662
00:29:46,772 --> 00:29:49,230
and the receptive fields gets bigger and bigger and bigger.

663
00:29:49,230 --> 00:29:51,530
And that location in the image aggregates information

664
00:29:51,530 --> 00:29:52,780
from farther and farther away.

665
00:29:52,780 --> 00:29:54,410
It's the exact same in a graph net.

666
00:29:54,410 --> 00:29:57,770
If I update a node based on its neighbors,

667
00:29:57,770 --> 00:30:01,310
then if I repeat that operation, I will-- every time I repeat it,

668
00:30:01,310 --> 00:30:03,297
I will hop one more neighbor away,

669
00:30:03,297 --> 00:30:05,130
and eventually, I'll cover the entire graph,

670
00:30:05,130 --> 00:30:06,732
and I'll globalize.

671
00:30:06,732 --> 00:30:08,690
Another idea that's shared between these things

672
00:30:08,690 --> 00:30:10,810
is that just like a ConvNet is chopping up

673
00:30:10,810 --> 00:30:13,330
the image into patches and operating on each patch

674
00:30:13,330 --> 00:30:16,030
independently and identically with the same parameters,

675
00:30:16,030 --> 00:30:18,930
a graph net will also chop up graphs into little local regions

676
00:30:18,930 --> 00:30:23,030
and operate on each local region independently and identically.

677
00:30:23,030 --> 00:30:27,630
And it's going to share the same parameters to update

678
00:30:27,630 --> 00:30:30,812
each region that-- each region of that graph.

679
00:30:30,812 --> 00:30:32,270
And another interesting property is

680
00:30:32,270 --> 00:30:34,630
that ConvNets can operate functionally

681
00:30:34,630 --> 00:30:36,010
on any size input image.

682
00:30:36,010 --> 00:30:38,790
They just slide that filter over as much as they need to go.

683
00:30:38,790 --> 00:30:41,410
And a graph that can operate on any size graph.

684
00:30:41,410 --> 00:30:43,830
You just continue running that local operation

685
00:30:43,830 --> 00:30:45,590
at every node in the graph.

686
00:30:45,590 --> 00:30:48,310
So that's a big difference from MLPs, which require

687
00:30:48,310 --> 00:30:49,710
a fixed dimensional input.

688
00:30:49,710 --> 00:30:50,650
OK, one question.

689
00:30:50,650 --> 00:30:52,150
AUDIENCE: Are we sharing the weights

690
00:30:52,150 --> 00:30:54,332
across different depths or only on [INAUDIBLE]?

691
00:30:54,332 --> 00:30:55,790
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so we're going

692
00:30:55,790 --> 00:30:56,890
to get into that in a minute.

693
00:30:56,890 --> 00:30:58,670
So are we sharing the weights across depth?

694
00:30:58,670 --> 00:31:00,170
And the answer is, that's an option.

695
00:31:00,170 --> 00:31:01,670
You can, or you don't have to.

696
00:31:01,670 --> 00:31:04,390
But we'll see that in just a second.

697
00:31:04,390 --> 00:31:06,710
So here's one more, just pointing out

698
00:31:06,710 --> 00:31:08,690
the exact relationship between a graph.

699
00:31:08,690 --> 00:31:10,430
Net and a convolutional network.

700
00:31:10,430 --> 00:31:16,510
So this is a CNN visualized like a graph net,

701
00:31:16,510 --> 00:31:18,710
so every single node in the graph

702
00:31:18,710 --> 00:31:21,250
is going to be connected to its neighbors,

703
00:31:21,250 --> 00:31:25,050
and every node will have this gray--

704
00:31:25,050 --> 00:31:27,210
let me try to get my mouse back.

705
00:31:27,210 --> 00:31:30,757
OK, there we go.

706
00:31:30,757 --> 00:31:32,590
I don't know why my mouse is not showing up.

707
00:31:35,210 --> 00:31:36,990
Anyway, It's fine.

708
00:31:36,990 --> 00:31:40,990
So the gray columns are meant to be the node attribute vectors.

709
00:31:40,990 --> 00:31:42,930
But what would the name of those gray columns

710
00:31:42,930 --> 00:31:47,078
be if we were working with a CNN?

711
00:31:47,078 --> 00:31:47,870
Anyone want to say?

712
00:31:47,870 --> 00:31:48,830
I think it's on the slide.

713
00:31:48,830 --> 00:31:50,250
AUDIENCE: Embeddings [INAUDIBLE].

714
00:31:50,250 --> 00:31:52,083
PHILLIP ISOLA: Embeddings or channels, yeah.

715
00:31:52,083 --> 00:31:54,610
So in a CNN, every point in the grid

716
00:31:54,610 --> 00:31:56,990
has this column of channels underneath it,

717
00:31:56,990 --> 00:32:00,210
and that's equivalent to the node vectors in a graph neural

718
00:32:00,210 --> 00:32:01,130
network.

719
00:32:01,130 --> 00:32:04,770
So maybe some CNN review questions very quickly.

720
00:32:04,770 --> 00:32:09,755
In this convolutional network, what is the kernel size?

721
00:32:09,755 --> 00:32:10,513
AUDIENCE: 3 by 3.

722
00:32:10,513 --> 00:32:11,430
PHILLIP ISOLA: 3 by 3.

723
00:32:11,430 --> 00:32:12,610
OK, great.

724
00:32:12,610 --> 00:32:13,950
And what is the stride?

725
00:32:17,210 --> 00:32:18,280
The stride is 3.

726
00:32:18,280 --> 00:32:19,780
So why is stride 3?

727
00:32:19,780 --> 00:32:23,040
Because I didn't draw edges between--

728
00:32:28,260 --> 00:32:30,038
if you look at the pattern of edges,

729
00:32:30,038 --> 00:32:32,080
it's like I'm taking that pattern, that template,

730
00:32:32,080 --> 00:32:38,220
and moving it over by 3 to get the next filter's application.

731
00:32:38,220 --> 00:32:40,080
OK, cool.

732
00:32:40,080 --> 00:32:43,220
So CNNs are GNNs.

733
00:32:43,220 --> 00:32:45,100
But now let's look at GNNs more generally

734
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:48,340
and get into the exact equations of how they operate.

735
00:32:48,340 --> 00:32:53,660
So the way I'm going to present this is that there's two steps.

736
00:32:53,660 --> 00:32:59,900
One step is we're going to pass messages

737
00:32:59,900 --> 00:33:02,380
from each node to its neighbors.

738
00:33:02,380 --> 00:33:04,380
And the next step is we're going to aggregate

739
00:33:04,380 --> 00:33:08,460
all of those messages to update the representation of each node.

740
00:33:08,460 --> 00:33:11,440
So every node has a vector associated with it,

741
00:33:11,440 --> 00:33:15,180
and that vector's going to be modified layer

742
00:33:15,180 --> 00:33:17,157
by layer through my graph net in order

743
00:33:17,157 --> 00:33:19,240
to come up with a better embedding that then makes

744
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:20,740
a prediction at the very end of the day,

745
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:22,365
just like in a ConvNet, how every layer

746
00:33:22,365 --> 00:33:24,000
modifies the set of channels underneath

747
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:25,300
every spatial location.

748
00:33:28,280 --> 00:33:32,220
So here's the general form of one layer application of a graph

749
00:33:32,220 --> 00:33:33,065
net.

750
00:33:33,065 --> 00:33:34,940
So going to look at a really tiny graph here.

751
00:33:34,940 --> 00:33:37,280
I'm going to highlight that middle node

752
00:33:37,280 --> 00:33:40,280
and ask, how does that middle nodes node vector get

753
00:33:40,280 --> 00:33:44,080
updated after one application--

754
00:33:44,080 --> 00:33:45,460
one layer of processing?

755
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:48,160
So what does one layer of processing-- so we call this--

756
00:33:48,160 --> 00:33:51,660
in each round k, in each layer k, we're going to do two things.

757
00:33:51,660 --> 00:33:53,680
We're going to aggregate and then update.

758
00:33:53,680 --> 00:33:55,920
So the aggregate function is going

759
00:33:55,920 --> 00:33:59,560
to be a function that takes the node vectors denoted

760
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:02,120
as h of all of my neighbors, and it's

761
00:34:02,120 --> 00:34:08,420
going to produce a message, m, from that set.

762
00:34:08,420 --> 00:34:09,880
And importantly, aggregate is going

763
00:34:09,880 --> 00:34:13,679
to be a function that is permutation-invariant.

764
00:34:13,679 --> 00:34:17,600
So if I reindex into my neighbors,

765
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:20,940
then I shouldn't get any different output message, m.

766
00:34:20,940 --> 00:34:24,739
OK, so the italic n is the set of neighbors

767
00:34:24,739 --> 00:34:30,020
of a vector of a node v. And I'm going

768
00:34:30,020 --> 00:34:34,580
to take the attribute vectors, h, from all my neighbors

769
00:34:34,580 --> 00:34:35,860
in that set.

770
00:34:35,860 --> 00:34:38,942
And I'm going to then somehow aggregate them.

771
00:34:38,942 --> 00:34:40,400
Now, once I've gotten that message,

772
00:34:40,400 --> 00:34:43,080
which is now just a vector-- it's a D-dimensional vector,

773
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:44,380
let's say.

774
00:34:44,380 --> 00:34:49,739
Then I will update the red node, node vector,

775
00:34:49,739 --> 00:34:52,007
using another function called Update.

776
00:34:52,007 --> 00:34:53,840
So I'll take the messages from my neighbors.

777
00:34:53,840 --> 00:34:55,548
My neighbors told me something about what

778
00:34:55,548 --> 00:34:57,860
they know about the graph and about the world.

779
00:34:57,860 --> 00:34:59,300
And I'll combine that information

780
00:34:59,300 --> 00:35:02,700
with what I know about myself to update my own representation.

781
00:35:02,700 --> 00:35:05,500
And now I can repeat this process k times.

782
00:35:05,500 --> 00:35:08,500
And as k gets larger, it's like going in depth

783
00:35:08,500 --> 00:35:11,217
in a neural network where I'm just updating my representation

784
00:35:11,217 --> 00:35:13,300
to be better and better and better, until finally,

785
00:35:13,300 --> 00:35:14,740
at the end, I make a prediction.

786
00:35:18,704 --> 00:35:23,120
So every application of those two steps

787
00:35:23,120 --> 00:35:26,680
is going to essentially increase my receptive field by one.

788
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:29,200
So that red node is going to get information

789
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:32,353
about the green nodes if I run this algorithm twice.

790
00:35:32,353 --> 00:35:34,520
So I get a message that's passed from the green node

791
00:35:34,520 --> 00:35:37,260
to the blue node, now to the red node on the second round.

792
00:35:39,920 --> 00:35:42,200
So some of you will be familiar with message

793
00:35:42,200 --> 00:35:47,200
passing algorithms and hidden Markov chains and so forth.

794
00:35:47,200 --> 00:35:49,180
And that's one perspective on this.

795
00:35:49,180 --> 00:35:52,400
But I'll try to give a few different perspectives.

796
00:35:52,400 --> 00:35:56,400
So what is the aggregate function, and what

797
00:35:56,400 --> 00:35:58,480
is the update function?

798
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:00,400
OK, let's make it a little bit more precise.

799
00:36:00,400 --> 00:36:05,100
So the aggregate function is going to be any set function.

800
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:07,600
That means it takes as input a set of items where

801
00:36:07,600 --> 00:36:08,940
the ordering doesn't matter.

802
00:36:08,940 --> 00:36:11,480
So it's permutation-invariant to the ordering.

803
00:36:11,480 --> 00:36:14,540
And it produces an output vector.

804
00:36:14,540 --> 00:36:16,340
Now, technically, we would call this

805
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:21,580
a multiset function because the set of inputs, which

806
00:36:21,580 --> 00:36:26,477
are the node vectors, h, could potentially have duplicates.

807
00:36:26,477 --> 00:36:28,060
So multiset is just the technical name

808
00:36:28,060 --> 00:36:31,148
for a set that can potentially have duplicate items.

809
00:36:31,148 --> 00:36:33,440
So if you come across this in the reading and so forth,

810
00:36:33,440 --> 00:36:35,000
they'll call it a multiset function.

811
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:37,240
But unless you're deep into set theory,

812
00:36:37,240 --> 00:36:40,340
then it's just a function from a set of items to a vector.

813
00:36:40,340 --> 00:36:44,420
The set of items can have duplicates.

814
00:36:44,420 --> 00:36:47,920
So let's look at some examples of aggregate functions.

815
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:50,140
So one example would be summation.

816
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:53,260
A summation takes a set of any length,

817
00:36:53,260 --> 00:36:55,660
and it produces a vector.

818
00:36:55,660 --> 00:36:58,160
So summation-- and also is permutation-invariant.

819
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:00,460
It doesn't matter how you order the items in that set.

820
00:37:00,460 --> 00:37:03,580
So summation is a multiset function that

821
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:08,380
produces a vector m as output.

822
00:37:08,380 --> 00:37:12,550
And that will be the message that is passed to the red node.

823
00:37:12,550 --> 00:37:14,290
And another one could be average.

824
00:37:14,290 --> 00:37:16,810
So on the left here, I have summation.

825
00:37:16,810 --> 00:37:19,670
But then in gray, I'm dividing by the number of neighbors, So.

826
00:37:19,670 --> 00:37:21,590
Would be the average, as opposed to the sum.

827
00:37:21,590 --> 00:37:23,257
It has interesting different properties.

828
00:37:23,257 --> 00:37:24,630
We'll get into that in a bit.

829
00:37:24,630 --> 00:37:28,090
And on the right is just another popular aggregation function,

830
00:37:28,090 --> 00:37:32,390
which is normalizing by the number of neighbors

831
00:37:32,390 --> 00:37:36,470
that both you and what are the messages coming from have.

832
00:37:36,470 --> 00:37:40,590
So just another variation on the same thing.

833
00:37:40,590 --> 00:37:43,430
So the key property is that aggregate is not

834
00:37:43,430 --> 00:37:45,970
a universal function, and it can't represent everything.

835
00:37:45,970 --> 00:37:47,130
Aggregate is constrained.

836
00:37:47,130 --> 00:37:51,890
Aggregate is a multiset function that's permutation-invariant.

837
00:37:51,890 --> 00:37:55,870
It maps a set of items to a vector,

838
00:37:55,870 --> 00:37:59,910
and the ordering of the set doesn't matter.

839
00:37:59,910 --> 00:38:02,510
Another interesting aggregate function

840
00:38:02,510 --> 00:38:06,130
that satisfies those rules is max or min.

841
00:38:06,130 --> 00:38:08,270
This is a lot like the pooling operations

842
00:38:08,270 --> 00:38:10,370
in convolutional networks.

843
00:38:10,370 --> 00:38:16,170
So max will just take the largest item in the set.

844
00:38:16,170 --> 00:38:19,893
It produces a fixed-length vector

845
00:38:19,893 --> 00:38:21,310
regardless of the size of the set.

846
00:38:21,310 --> 00:38:23,450
So it's a multiset function.

847
00:38:23,450 --> 00:38:26,032
Min, same property.

848
00:38:26,032 --> 00:38:27,490
And there's some interesting things

849
00:38:27,490 --> 00:38:28,865
you can do when you start to look

850
00:38:28,865 --> 00:38:30,790
at different aggregate functions.

851
00:38:30,790 --> 00:38:34,170
So here's a graph algorithm that can be implemented

852
00:38:34,170 --> 00:38:36,330
with min aggregation.

853
00:38:36,330 --> 00:38:40,490
And this is the shortest-path Bellman-Ford algorithm.

854
00:38:40,490 --> 00:38:43,550
So a lot of you will have seen this in Intro to Algorithms.

855
00:38:43,550 --> 00:38:46,650
But in case you don't remember, to find the shortest

856
00:38:46,650 --> 00:38:48,410
path with Bellman-Ford, what you do

857
00:38:48,410 --> 00:38:51,490
is you assign to every node in your graph

858
00:38:51,490 --> 00:38:53,730
an attribute vector, which is just

859
00:38:53,730 --> 00:38:57,152
going to be the distance to the target node

860
00:38:57,152 --> 00:38:58,110
you're trying to reach.

861
00:38:58,110 --> 00:38:59,870
So I'm trying to find the shortest path to the target.

862
00:38:59,870 --> 00:39:01,472
So every node will have an attribute,

863
00:39:01,472 --> 00:39:02,430
which is just a scalar.

864
00:39:02,430 --> 00:39:03,970
It's just the distance--

865
00:39:03,970 --> 00:39:07,050
my estimate of the shortest distance or the cost

866
00:39:07,050 --> 00:39:10,270
of the shortest path to get to the target,

867
00:39:10,270 --> 00:39:15,590
and I'll initialize those vectors, d, with infinity.

868
00:39:15,590 --> 00:39:20,190
And then I will run this recurrence over and over

869
00:39:20,190 --> 00:39:22,070
again until I converge, where I'm

870
00:39:22,070 --> 00:39:25,830
trying to say- I will say that the attribute

871
00:39:25,830 --> 00:39:31,190
vector for the target node is 0, and everything else is infinity.

872
00:39:31,190 --> 00:39:34,110
And then as I run this, I'll look at all of my neighbors,

873
00:39:34,110 --> 00:39:41,790
and I will see if the distance between myself and the neighbor,

874
00:39:41,790 --> 00:39:43,790
plus the estimate that the neighbor has

875
00:39:43,790 --> 00:39:45,152
of the distance to the target--

876
00:39:45,152 --> 00:39:47,610
I'll see what that is, and I'll take the minimum over that.

877
00:39:47,610 --> 00:39:49,277
And that will give me the shortest path.

878
00:39:49,277 --> 00:39:52,950
If I kind of propagate out, now, from that target,

879
00:39:52,950 --> 00:39:54,670
these distances initially it will be 0,

880
00:39:54,670 --> 00:39:56,003
and everything will be infinity.

881
00:39:56,003 --> 00:39:58,030
And then all the neighbors of that target

882
00:39:58,030 --> 00:40:01,948
will be 1, if the cost is 1 to go one hop in the graph.

883
00:40:01,948 --> 00:40:03,490
And then this will propagate outward,

884
00:40:03,490 --> 00:40:05,070
and this will tell me the shortest--

885
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:07,210
the cost of the shortest path to that target.

886
00:40:07,210 --> 00:40:08,330
Yeah.

887
00:40:08,330 --> 00:40:10,410
AUDIENCE: So because the aggregate function

888
00:40:10,410 --> 00:40:12,250
is constrained, how--

889
00:40:12,250 --> 00:40:14,970
I guess this is more of a question of designing a problem

890
00:40:14,970 --> 00:40:16,510
to then use a graph--

891
00:40:16,510 --> 00:40:17,930
a graph network.

892
00:40:17,930 --> 00:40:21,170
How do you know that your information needs

893
00:40:21,170 --> 00:40:22,590
to be permutation-invariant?

894
00:40:22,590 --> 00:40:24,310
Or how do you classify a set of problems

895
00:40:24,310 --> 00:40:27,425
to where you say, OK, I need to just aggregate

896
00:40:27,425 --> 00:40:29,050
my information together, and it doesn't

897
00:40:29,050 --> 00:40:31,370
matter what order they come in?

898
00:40:31,370 --> 00:40:33,790
How do when to classify a problem like that?

899
00:40:33,790 --> 00:40:35,290
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the question

900
00:40:35,290 --> 00:40:38,810
is, how do when you want permutation invariance?

901
00:40:38,810 --> 00:40:40,310
And I think that's a good question.

902
00:40:40,310 --> 00:40:42,510
It's going to depend a lot on the domain.

903
00:40:45,690 --> 00:40:51,730
But yeah, so any problem where the ordering of inputs

904
00:40:51,730 --> 00:40:54,513
doesn't change the optimal thing you want to do,

905
00:40:54,513 --> 00:40:55,930
the prediction you want to make is

906
00:40:55,930 --> 00:40:57,610
going to be something you want to apply

907
00:40:57,610 --> 00:41:00,410
this constraint to being permutation-invariant to.

908
00:41:00,410 --> 00:41:02,210
And we're seeing a lot of examples

909
00:41:02,210 --> 00:41:04,510
where, structurally, that actually does make sense.

910
00:41:04,510 --> 00:41:08,900
Like, the shortest path in the graph has that property.

911
00:41:08,900 --> 00:41:12,900
I think that maybe a clearer example of a case where

912
00:41:12,900 --> 00:41:15,220
you wouldn't want permutation invariance

913
00:41:15,220 --> 00:41:17,860
is if I'm doing image processing,

914
00:41:17,860 --> 00:41:19,940
and I know that the top of the image

915
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:23,020
is more likely to be sky than the bottom of the image.

916
00:41:23,020 --> 00:41:26,740
If I have a graph which has these local patches

917
00:41:26,740 --> 00:41:29,740
that I'm processing, I want to know where I am in the image.

918
00:41:29,740 --> 00:41:32,860
And I think Sarah talked about adding positional encoding

919
00:41:32,860 --> 00:41:35,220
to tell you where you are in an image

920
00:41:35,220 --> 00:41:38,160
if you don't want this translation invariance.

921
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:41,360
So translation invariance is one type of permutation invariance,

922
00:41:41,360 --> 00:41:45,380
but permutation invariance is a more general version of that.

923
00:41:45,380 --> 00:41:46,100
OK.

924
00:41:46,100 --> 00:41:46,725
Yeah, question.

925
00:41:46,725 --> 00:41:48,475
AUDIENCE: Yeah, quick clarifying question.

926
00:41:48,475 --> 00:41:51,180
You mentioned the output of the aggregate function is a vector.

927
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:54,780
Can you just quickly just say what the dimensions

928
00:41:54,780 --> 00:41:55,860
of that vector are?

929
00:41:55,860 --> 00:41:58,360
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the output of the aggregate function

930
00:41:58,360 --> 00:42:00,280
is a D-dimensional column vector.

931
00:42:04,840 --> 00:42:06,520
So yeah, on your problem set, you'll

932
00:42:06,520 --> 00:42:09,220
have some questions about this shortest-path computation.

933
00:42:09,220 --> 00:42:11,220
So you'll get a little bit more intuition of it.

934
00:42:11,220 --> 00:42:13,760
But it's just a construction to demonstrate

935
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:18,720
that you can represent classical graph problems, like graph

936
00:42:18,720 --> 00:42:21,800
search and shortest path problems, with these aggregate

937
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:23,180
and update operations.

938
00:42:23,180 --> 00:42:25,360
In this particular case, Bellman-Ford

939
00:42:25,360 --> 00:42:28,380
can be implemented just as a GNN that only does aggregate,

940
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:30,840
and the update is trivial in this case.

941
00:42:30,840 --> 00:42:33,793
So Bellman-Ford is on the left, and the equivalent graph net

942
00:42:33,793 --> 00:42:34,460
is on the right.

943
00:42:34,460 --> 00:42:37,160
The graph net-- the aggregate is going

944
00:42:37,160 --> 00:42:40,840
to be a min for Bellman-Ford rather than the sum.

945
00:42:40,840 --> 00:42:47,020
And the computation of-- the node embedding of your neighbor

946
00:42:47,020 --> 00:42:49,500
plus this cost could be represented as an MLP,

947
00:42:49,500 --> 00:42:50,938
which just adds 1--

948
00:42:50,938 --> 00:42:52,480
adds the cost of 1, if we're assuming

949
00:42:52,480 --> 00:42:56,040
that every edge in the graph has length 1.

950
00:42:56,040 --> 00:42:58,640
So you'll work through that a little bit more in your problem

951
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:00,680
set.

952
00:43:00,680 --> 00:43:04,800
So one question you might have is, well,

953
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:06,960
sum seems like a fine aggregation function.

954
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:08,780
Min seems like a fine one.

955
00:43:08,780 --> 00:43:11,100
Is there a family of aggregation functions

956
00:43:11,100 --> 00:43:14,980
that are universal in the sense that any multiset permutation

957
00:43:14,980 --> 00:43:20,100
invariant mapping can be represented in that family?

958
00:43:20,100 --> 00:43:23,940
And the answer is that, yes, this family

959
00:43:23,940 --> 00:43:27,660
of aggregation functions is universal in that sense.

960
00:43:27,660 --> 00:43:36,020
So take my neighbor's node embedding or my neighbor's

961
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:38,540
node vector, and take my own node vector,

962
00:43:38,540 --> 00:43:40,740
pass those through an MLP, then sum up

963
00:43:40,740 --> 00:43:43,600
over- sum of that operation over all of my neighbors,

964
00:43:43,600 --> 00:43:45,860
and then pass the output through another MLP.

965
00:43:45,860 --> 00:43:48,700
This is a universal approximator of the family

966
00:43:48,700 --> 00:43:52,220
of multiset functions.

967
00:43:52,220 --> 00:43:54,840
And one question you might have is, wait a second.

968
00:43:54,840 --> 00:43:57,740
Didn't we say that MLPs on their own are universal approximators?

969
00:43:57,740 --> 00:44:02,320
And they are if I increase the width to infinity.

970
00:44:02,320 --> 00:44:05,820
But here we're, not trying to approximate all functions.

971
00:44:05,820 --> 00:44:07,680
We're trying to only approximate-- we

972
00:44:07,680 --> 00:44:10,640
want to have an approximator that can't fit functions that

973
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:13,640
are not multiset functions.

974
00:44:13,640 --> 00:44:15,693
So the sum is the thing that makes it

975
00:44:15,693 --> 00:44:17,360
so it's one of these multiset functions,

976
00:44:17,360 --> 00:44:20,658
that the ordering of the edges-- sorry,

977
00:44:20,658 --> 00:44:22,200
the ordering of the neighbors doesn't

978
00:44:22,200 --> 00:44:25,720
matter because we're just going to summate over them.

979
00:44:25,720 --> 00:44:30,200
So this has the property that it is a universal approximator

980
00:44:30,200 --> 00:44:32,100
to the family of multiset functions,

981
00:44:32,100 --> 00:44:37,120
but it can't represent other functions.

982
00:44:37,120 --> 00:44:37,980
So that's good.

983
00:44:37,980 --> 00:44:41,000
It's a constrained family.

984
00:44:41,000 --> 00:44:45,180
The other thing that we need is what we call an update function,

985
00:44:45,180 --> 00:44:47,600
and the update function will take our messages

986
00:44:47,600 --> 00:44:51,320
from our neighbors and combine them with our current node

987
00:44:51,320 --> 00:44:53,320
vector and update that node vector

988
00:44:53,320 --> 00:44:56,640
to be a better representation of the graph,

989
00:44:56,640 --> 00:44:59,080
of the information that we're trying to process.

990
00:44:59,080 --> 00:45:02,070
And for this one, we're just going to use a very--

991
00:45:02,070 --> 00:45:05,850
a simple update, which is going to be an MLP, just

992
00:45:05,850 --> 00:45:07,830
a tiny, one-layer MLP.

993
00:45:07,830 --> 00:45:12,598
It goes linear transformation of my each of my neighbors.

994
00:45:12,598 --> 00:45:14,390
Oh, sorry, linear transformation of myself,

995
00:45:14,390 --> 00:45:16,650
plus linear transformation of the message coming

996
00:45:16,650 --> 00:45:18,807
from my neighbors, plus a bias, pass

997
00:45:18,807 --> 00:45:21,390
through sigma, which is going to be a pointwise non-linearity.

998
00:45:21,390 --> 00:45:22,770
That could be a ReLU.

999
00:45:22,770 --> 00:45:26,130
That's a canonical update function.

1000
00:45:26,130 --> 00:45:29,070
So once we have those two things,

1001
00:45:29,070 --> 00:45:30,130
then we have a graph net.

1002
00:45:36,610 --> 00:45:39,143
That will give me updated node embeddings,

1003
00:45:39,143 --> 00:45:40,810
and I can apply that over and over again

1004
00:45:40,810 --> 00:45:45,050
to get my node embeddings into a better and better state.

1005
00:45:45,050 --> 00:45:46,550
And then at the very end of the day,

1006
00:45:46,550 --> 00:45:48,175
I could just take those node embeddings

1007
00:45:48,175 --> 00:45:50,270
as my prediction about properties of the nodes.

1008
00:45:50,270 --> 00:45:52,330
But if I want to instead predict something

1009
00:45:52,330 --> 00:45:55,070
about the entire graph, I just do one more step,

1010
00:45:55,070 --> 00:45:58,170
which is very simple, which is I aggregate the node embeddings

1011
00:45:58,170 --> 00:46:00,570
into a final prediction.

1012
00:46:00,570 --> 00:46:04,050
So here is my mapping from a graph to an embedding,

1013
00:46:04,050 --> 00:46:07,770
and it requires this final step, which we call readout.

1014
00:46:07,770 --> 00:46:09,270
There's other ways that you could've

1015
00:46:09,270 --> 00:46:11,827
decided on the conventions of how to break down

1016
00:46:11,827 --> 00:46:12,910
the pieces of a graph net.

1017
00:46:12,910 --> 00:46:14,430
I'm just giving you one that makes

1018
00:46:14,430 --> 00:46:17,350
some of the analysis simple and clean.

1019
00:46:17,350 --> 00:46:19,270
But we're going to define this extra layer

1020
00:46:19,270 --> 00:46:22,313
at the end, which is going to take all of my node embeddings.

1021
00:46:22,313 --> 00:46:24,230
So it's going to be a multiset function again.

1022
00:46:24,230 --> 00:46:26,210
It's going to take a set of node embeddings,

1023
00:46:26,210 --> 00:46:29,590
possibly with duplicates, and it's going to output a vector.

1024
00:46:29,590 --> 00:46:31,270
And this will be how I get a vector

1025
00:46:31,270 --> 00:46:34,110
embedding of an entire graph.

1026
00:46:34,110 --> 00:46:35,730
It's just another aggregate operator.

1027
00:46:35,730 --> 00:46:37,610
It has exactly the same form as aggregate,

1028
00:46:37,610 --> 00:46:38,910
but it's over all nodes in the graph,

1029
00:46:38,910 --> 00:46:40,530
as opposed to the neighbors of a node.

1030
00:46:45,330 --> 00:46:49,570
So this is the message-passing view of graph.

1031
00:46:49,570 --> 00:46:53,190
Net that connects to classical theory of message-passing

1032
00:46:53,190 --> 00:46:54,733
algorithms in graphs.

1033
00:46:54,733 --> 00:46:57,150
But I actually think that there's a better and simpler way

1034
00:46:57,150 --> 00:47:00,570
of understanding all of this, which

1035
00:47:00,570 --> 00:47:03,230
will connect very intimately to what we'll see soon,

1036
00:47:03,230 --> 00:47:06,410
which is transformers.

1037
00:47:06,410 --> 00:47:07,990
So here's how I like to think of it.

1038
00:47:07,990 --> 00:47:15,590
So if I unroll those message passing iterations for k steps,

1039
00:47:15,590 --> 00:47:22,210
I can just think of every step as taking a set of node vectors

1040
00:47:22,210 --> 00:47:24,490
and updating them.

1041
00:47:24,490 --> 00:47:27,030
And here's my set of node vectors at the input.

1042
00:47:27,030 --> 00:47:30,530
That's my input data, the observed data about the nodes

1043
00:47:30,530 --> 00:47:31,610
in the graph.

1044
00:47:31,610 --> 00:47:36,030
Again, if they're drugs, it might be the name of the drug.

1045
00:47:36,030 --> 00:47:39,450
If the graph is a molecule it might be the atom,

1046
00:47:39,450 --> 00:47:42,130
like is it carbon or is it hydrogen.

1047
00:47:42,130 --> 00:47:44,250
If it's a social network, then the input could

1048
00:47:44,250 --> 00:47:47,170
be a description of the user profile

1049
00:47:47,170 --> 00:47:49,810
for every person in that graph.

1050
00:47:49,810 --> 00:47:53,970
OK, I take that set of node vectors,

1051
00:47:53,970 --> 00:47:55,255
and now I will do aggregate.

1052
00:47:55,255 --> 00:47:56,630
So what is aggregate going to do?

1053
00:47:59,790 --> 00:48:06,430
I'm trying to make it so that the vertical aligned nodes are

1054
00:48:06,430 --> 00:48:07,650
corresponding to each other.

1055
00:48:07,650 --> 00:48:12,430
So my message that's going to be passed to the red node--

1056
00:48:12,430 --> 00:48:19,390
it's going to be that black vector, which is the second one.

1057
00:48:19,390 --> 00:48:22,750
And as you can see, the red node in the graph

1058
00:48:22,750 --> 00:48:26,510
gets input from the teal, the blue, and the yellow node.

1059
00:48:26,510 --> 00:48:28,870
So the message is going to be coming from them.

1060
00:48:28,870 --> 00:48:35,070
And aggregate is going to take all the node vectors

1061
00:48:35,070 --> 00:48:37,390
from those three neighbors and combine them

1062
00:48:37,390 --> 00:48:39,210
into a single D-dimensional vector.

1063
00:48:39,210 --> 00:48:42,810
And that's my message that will be used to update

1064
00:48:42,810 --> 00:48:45,350
the red nodes' node embedding.

1065
00:48:45,350 --> 00:48:47,245
And then I will run Update, which

1066
00:48:47,245 --> 00:48:49,870
is going to take the red nodes' node embedding plus the message

1067
00:48:49,870 --> 00:48:53,590
from those neighbors and provide an updated

1068
00:48:53,590 --> 00:48:55,630
representation of the red node.

1069
00:48:55,630 --> 00:48:56,990
So what does this look like?

1070
00:48:56,990 --> 00:49:01,035
This now looks a lot like a neural network MLP.

1071
00:49:01,035 --> 00:49:02,410
Just like we saw before, we would

1072
00:49:02,410 --> 00:49:04,850
have a set of neurons as input, and then we

1073
00:49:04,850 --> 00:49:07,690
would take a linear combination, some aggregation of them.

1074
00:49:07,690 --> 00:49:09,710
And then we would do a pointwise nonlinearity.

1075
00:49:09,710 --> 00:49:13,290
Now we have a set of vectors as input.

1076
00:49:13,290 --> 00:49:15,730
For every output vector, we're going

1077
00:49:15,730 --> 00:49:18,050
to aggregate information over the neighbors

1078
00:49:18,050 --> 00:49:19,030
that it connects to.

1079
00:49:19,030 --> 00:49:21,592
And we're going to do a pointwise non-linearity,

1080
00:49:21,592 --> 00:49:23,550
but it's going to be a node-wise non-linearity.

1081
00:49:23,550 --> 00:49:25,690
It's going to be this update function which

1082
00:49:25,690 --> 00:49:28,610
will apply the same vector-to-vector mapping

1083
00:49:28,610 --> 00:49:32,970
to every single node in our graph.

1084
00:49:32,970 --> 00:49:34,770
And then we'll repeat the process again

1085
00:49:34,770 --> 00:49:37,950
on the next step of this message-passing algorithm.

1086
00:49:37,950 --> 00:49:42,050
But if we unroll it like this, it just looks like an MLP.

1087
00:49:42,050 --> 00:49:47,730
The only difference is that the neurons in this MLP

1088
00:49:47,730 --> 00:49:49,510
are going to be vectors.

1089
00:49:49,510 --> 00:49:51,170
They're going to be node embeddings.

1090
00:49:51,170 --> 00:49:53,610
And that means that the pointwise operation will

1091
00:49:53,610 --> 00:49:55,240
be a vector-to-vector mapping.

1092
00:49:55,240 --> 00:49:58,820
The aggregate operation will be a vector-- a set of vectors

1093
00:49:58,820 --> 00:50:00,980
to a vector operation.

1094
00:50:00,980 --> 00:50:03,900
But topologically, it looks very similar to just a regular old

1095
00:50:03,900 --> 00:50:05,020
neural network.

1096
00:50:05,020 --> 00:50:07,200
OK, a few questions.

1097
00:50:07,200 --> 00:50:07,917
Let's go here.

1098
00:50:07,917 --> 00:50:10,500
AUDIENCE: How is this different from an attention head and how

1099
00:50:10,500 --> 00:50:11,780
it--

1100
00:50:11,780 --> 00:50:12,740
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1101
00:50:12,740 --> 00:50:15,060
So how is this different from an attention head?

1102
00:50:15,060 --> 00:50:19,140
So this perspective is the connection between graph nets

1103
00:50:19,140 --> 00:50:19,920
and transformers.

1104
00:50:19,920 --> 00:50:21,700
And transformers are a special kind of graph net,

1105
00:50:21,700 --> 00:50:23,360
so we're going to talk a lot more in detail about that,

1106
00:50:23,360 --> 00:50:24,860
and we'll get to attention then.

1107
00:50:24,860 --> 00:50:27,720
Attention is just a special aggregation operator.

1108
00:50:27,720 --> 00:50:30,600
It's a particular kind of aggregation operator.

1109
00:50:30,600 --> 00:50:34,323
AUDIENCE: Is update always nonlinear, and why?

1110
00:50:34,323 --> 00:50:35,740
PHILLIP ISOLA: So you need to have

1111
00:50:35,740 --> 00:50:37,320
some nonlinearities in here.

1112
00:50:37,320 --> 00:50:39,900
And in graph nets, you can have nonlinearities

1113
00:50:39,900 --> 00:50:42,100
in the aggregate and/or the update.

1114
00:50:42,100 --> 00:50:45,260
And I think typically, they'll both be nonlinear.

1115
00:50:45,260 --> 00:50:47,460
But theoretically, you probably can

1116
00:50:47,460 --> 00:50:50,425
prove universal approximation even if only one is nonlinear.

1117
00:50:50,425 --> 00:50:52,300
I have to think about that a little bit more.

1118
00:50:52,300 --> 00:50:54,600
Yeah.

1119
00:50:54,600 --> 00:50:57,960
AUDIENCE: So [INAUDIBLE] the optimal number of iterations

1120
00:50:57,960 --> 00:50:59,560
per k, which is [INAUDIBLE] k?

1121
00:50:59,560 --> 00:51:01,893
PHILLIP ISOLA: So is the question, how should you set k?

1122
00:51:01,893 --> 00:51:03,080
What is the optimal k?

1123
00:51:03,080 --> 00:51:06,080
Yeah, so typically we will--

1124
00:51:06,080 --> 00:51:07,440
k is like your depth.

1125
00:51:07,440 --> 00:51:09,840
Now, in graph nets, the interesting thing

1126
00:51:09,840 --> 00:51:13,060
is that we could choose to share parameters.

1127
00:51:13,060 --> 00:51:15,400
We could choose to do the same message-passing algorithm

1128
00:51:15,400 --> 00:51:17,000
at every iteration.

1129
00:51:17,000 --> 00:51:20,880
Or we could choose to say that my aggregate function has

1130
00:51:20,880 --> 00:51:23,800
the superscript k, so I use a different set of parameters

1131
00:51:23,800 --> 00:51:28,160
for every iteration of that aggregate function.

1132
00:51:28,160 --> 00:51:31,140
Now, aggregate is going to be some parametrized function, psi,

1133
00:51:31,140 --> 00:51:31,640
here.

1134
00:51:31,640 --> 00:51:36,760
We said it could be in this form of an MLP.

1135
00:51:36,760 --> 00:51:40,602
And we could choose to make it time-dependent, dependent on k,

1136
00:51:40,602 --> 00:51:42,060
and use different parameters per k.

1137
00:51:42,060 --> 00:51:44,000
Or we can choose to make it--

1138
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:46,140
use the same parameters for every single layer.

1139
00:51:46,140 --> 00:51:48,740
If we choose to make it time-dependent,

1140
00:51:48,740 --> 00:51:52,060
then we'll have to pick a fixed depth, k,

1141
00:51:52,060 --> 00:51:54,400
that we're going to unroll to and train for that depth

1142
00:51:54,400 --> 00:51:56,420
because we'll have a different set of parameters

1143
00:51:56,420 --> 00:51:58,280
for each of these operations.

1144
00:51:58,280 --> 00:52:01,220
But if we choose to make it not depend on k and share parameters

1145
00:52:01,220 --> 00:52:04,700
across time, across depth, then you

1146
00:52:04,700 --> 00:52:06,880
can just run this as long as you want,

1147
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:08,660
and it will keep doing computation.

1148
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:10,467
And you might want to ensure that there's

1149
00:52:10,467 --> 00:52:12,800
good asymptotic properties, like, if you run it forever,

1150
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:13,840
it will converge to a good value.

1151
00:52:13,840 --> 00:52:15,548
And those are two different settings that

1152
00:52:15,548 --> 00:52:17,740
can be interesting to look at.

1153
00:52:17,740 --> 00:52:20,060
Let's go in the back over here.

1154
00:52:20,060 --> 00:52:22,620
AUDIENCE: Can you explain the intuition behind the update

1155
00:52:22,620 --> 00:52:23,380
formula again?

1156
00:52:23,380 --> 00:52:25,100
And specifically, I'm curious why

1157
00:52:25,100 --> 00:52:28,300
we use different weight matrices for the self

1158
00:52:28,300 --> 00:52:29,900
and the neighborhoods.

1159
00:52:29,900 --> 00:52:34,020
PHILLIP ISOLA: So what is the intuition behind the update

1160
00:52:34,020 --> 00:52:35,500
formula?

1161
00:52:35,500 --> 00:52:37,332
Why do we use different weight matrices?

1162
00:52:37,332 --> 00:52:39,540
So I think what you could do is you could always just

1163
00:52:39,540 --> 00:52:44,900
concatenate h, your node embedding, with the message

1164
00:52:44,900 --> 00:52:48,160
from the neighbors, with m, and then have one big W.

1165
00:52:48,160 --> 00:52:52,600
And then it will just look like a linear followed by ReLU layer.

1166
00:52:52,600 --> 00:52:58,480
It's just separated here for notational clarity, I suppose.

1167
00:52:58,480 --> 00:53:00,680
OK, one more, and then I'll move on.

1168
00:53:00,680 --> 00:53:01,818
Yeah.

1169
00:53:01,818 --> 00:53:03,360
AUDIENCE: How do you pass information

1170
00:53:03,360 --> 00:53:06,280
if you have edge information, for example, the distances

1171
00:53:06,280 --> 00:53:07,705
between the nodes?

1172
00:53:07,705 --> 00:53:09,580
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, that's a great question.

1173
00:53:09,580 --> 00:53:11,580
So what if you have edge information?

1174
00:53:11,580 --> 00:53:14,180
So I said you have node attributes,

1175
00:53:14,180 --> 00:53:16,920
and you just send messages based on which

1176
00:53:16,920 --> 00:53:18,755
edges connect to which nodes.

1177
00:53:18,755 --> 00:53:20,880
So the edges only tell you about who your neighbors

1178
00:53:20,880 --> 00:53:22,060
are to send messages to you.

1179
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:25,840
There's no other weight on the edge or information on the edge.

1180
00:53:25,840 --> 00:53:28,100
And so I won't get into it in detail,

1181
00:53:28,100 --> 00:53:31,840
but you can also have edge attribute vectors

1182
00:53:31,840 --> 00:53:34,560
and then have a generalization of a GNN

1183
00:53:34,560 --> 00:53:36,895
that has node attributes and edge attributes.

1184
00:53:36,895 --> 00:53:38,520
And then the update and the aggregation

1185
00:53:38,520 --> 00:53:41,600
is going to also take into account the edge attributes.

1186
00:53:41,600 --> 00:53:42,760
Yeah.

1187
00:53:42,760 --> 00:53:43,260
OK.

1188
00:53:48,640 --> 00:53:51,970
So this is the picture how I like to think about GNNs.

1189
00:53:51,970 --> 00:53:53,750
And you might be wondering, wait a second,

1190
00:53:53,750 --> 00:53:55,850
where is backprop coming in here?

1191
00:53:55,850 --> 00:53:57,070
Is this message passing?

1192
00:53:57,070 --> 00:53:58,630
Is that an optimization algorithm?

1193
00:53:58,630 --> 00:54:01,610
No, the message passing in this propagation-- that's the forward

1194
00:54:01,610 --> 00:54:02,890
pass through the network.

1195
00:54:02,890 --> 00:54:06,813
It's taking data about the graph and updating the representation

1196
00:54:06,813 --> 00:54:08,730
of the nodes over and over again until finally

1197
00:54:08,730 --> 00:54:11,083
having a readout at the end that makes a prediction.

1198
00:54:11,083 --> 00:54:12,250
So how would you train that?

1199
00:54:12,250 --> 00:54:15,210
You just backpropagate through this computation graph.

1200
00:54:15,210 --> 00:54:16,610
Nothing different.

1201
00:54:16,610 --> 00:54:20,092
So these message-passing steps are just a new computation graph

1202
00:54:20,092 --> 00:54:21,550
that looks like that when unrolled,

1203
00:54:21,550 --> 00:54:23,342
and I can backprop through the whole thing.

1204
00:54:23,342 --> 00:54:26,410
So same exact algorithm that we saw for training

1205
00:54:26,410 --> 00:54:28,930
any other neural network.

1206
00:54:28,930 --> 00:54:32,130
OK, cool.

1207
00:54:32,130 --> 00:54:36,450
OK, this might be a slightly tricky question.

1208
00:54:36,450 --> 00:54:39,930
But I said that convolutional networks

1209
00:54:39,930 --> 00:54:44,250
can be represented as a graph, and then you can observe--

1210
00:54:44,250 --> 00:54:46,010
you can just treat them like a graph net.

1211
00:54:46,010 --> 00:54:48,470
It's a graph that has this grid connectivity.

1212
00:54:48,470 --> 00:54:52,612
What is the graph net for an MLP?

1213
00:54:52,612 --> 00:54:53,570
What does it look like?

1214
00:54:53,570 --> 00:54:55,278
What does the graph look like for an MLP?

1215
00:54:58,670 --> 00:55:00,110
Somebody who hasn't spoken.

1216
00:55:00,110 --> 00:55:00,693
Did you speak?

1217
00:55:00,693 --> 00:55:01,193
No.

1218
00:55:01,193 --> 00:55:01,710
OK.

1219
00:55:01,710 --> 00:55:03,230
Yeah, in the back there.

1220
00:55:03,230 --> 00:55:05,238
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1221
00:55:05,238 --> 00:55:06,030
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

1222
00:55:06,030 --> 00:55:06,770
Just a chain.

1223
00:55:06,770 --> 00:55:08,770
So in particular, what does the graph look like?

1224
00:55:08,770 --> 00:55:12,650
It's really just a node because the message-passing algorithm--

1225
00:55:12,650 --> 00:55:15,910
so remember that an MLP takes a vector of neurons as input

1226
00:55:15,910 --> 00:55:18,830
and then does linear, [INAUDIBLE], nonlinear, linear.

1227
00:55:18,830 --> 00:55:21,490
So a node in a graph net comes with a vector.

1228
00:55:21,490 --> 00:55:23,830
So that has the generality of being the vector of inputs

1229
00:55:23,830 --> 00:55:25,190
to an MLP.

1230
00:55:25,190 --> 00:55:29,190
And then if we unroll the message-passing algorithm.

1231
00:55:29,190 --> 00:55:30,130
It's just this.

1232
00:55:30,130 --> 00:55:33,170
It's just a chain of, take that vector, update it.

1233
00:55:33,170 --> 00:55:35,068
And in this case, there's only one vector,

1234
00:55:35,068 --> 00:55:36,610
so the aggregate function is trivial.

1235
00:55:36,610 --> 00:55:37,870
It can be identity.

1236
00:55:37,870 --> 00:55:42,672
And the update we said is just W times my node vector, right?

1237
00:55:42,672 --> 00:55:44,130
Here's what we said for the update.

1238
00:55:44,130 --> 00:55:45,588
There's no neighbors, so it'll just

1239
00:55:45,588 --> 00:55:49,770
be W self times my node vector h, Pass through a ReLU

1240
00:55:49,770 --> 00:55:51,490
or a pointwise nonlinearity.

1241
00:55:51,490 --> 00:55:52,690
That's an MLP.

1242
00:55:52,690 --> 00:55:55,930
So an MLP is just a graph net that's a single node.

1243
00:55:55,930 --> 00:55:58,230
ConvNet is a graph net that is a grid.

1244
00:56:01,410 --> 00:56:01,910
OK.

1245
00:56:01,910 --> 00:56:03,850
So throughout this class I'm going

1246
00:56:03,850 --> 00:56:06,470
to try to point out connections between different things

1247
00:56:06,470 --> 00:56:08,290
because I think sometimes when you first

1248
00:56:08,290 --> 00:56:11,790
learn about deep learning and just any topic,

1249
00:56:11,790 --> 00:56:13,910
it'll seem like everything is different,

1250
00:56:13,910 --> 00:56:16,390
but really, everything is the same.

1251
00:56:16,390 --> 00:56:19,990
Really, everything is densely connected to all other concepts,

1252
00:56:19,990 --> 00:56:22,170
and it's just like-- you can cast

1253
00:56:22,170 --> 00:56:25,390
one concept as another concept with small manipulations.

1254
00:56:25,390 --> 00:56:28,090
And I find this really helps me to get inside and understand

1255
00:56:28,090 --> 00:56:29,410
how these things work.

1256
00:56:29,410 --> 00:56:31,393
OK.

1257
00:56:31,393 --> 00:56:33,310
So there was a question about edge attributes,

1258
00:56:33,310 --> 00:56:35,670
and I'm not going to go into details on that.

1259
00:56:35,670 --> 00:56:38,810
But yes, you can add vectors describing the information

1260
00:56:38,810 --> 00:56:41,490
that an edge carries as well.

1261
00:56:41,490 --> 00:56:46,390
We could have not just like a single message-passing

1262
00:56:46,390 --> 00:56:48,230
algorithm, but maybe k--

1263
00:56:48,230 --> 00:56:50,150
or n different message-passing algorithms

1264
00:56:50,150 --> 00:56:52,570
in parallel that are passing different types of messages.

1265
00:56:52,570 --> 00:56:56,390
There's a lot of variations and riffs on these general themes.

1266
00:56:56,390 --> 00:56:57,950
One of the most important ones is

1267
00:56:57,950 --> 00:57:02,030
that we can aggregate with a operation which is not just

1268
00:57:02,030 --> 00:57:03,630
summing or averaging, but instead

1269
00:57:03,630 --> 00:57:07,870
is called attention, which is like a summation that's

1270
00:57:07,870 --> 00:57:10,150
dependent on the value of your node embeddings.

1271
00:57:10,150 --> 00:57:12,150
We're going to talk a lot more detail about that

1272
00:57:12,150 --> 00:57:14,525
because the name for that system is called a transformer.

1273
00:57:14,525 --> 00:57:17,230
So transformers are graph nets with attention

1274
00:57:17,230 --> 00:57:19,550
as the aggregation operator.

1275
00:57:19,550 --> 00:57:22,330
And you can read up on some of these other variations, too,

1276
00:57:22,330 --> 00:57:24,910
but I'm not going to go into detail on them.

1277
00:57:24,910 --> 00:57:27,790
The reading that we assigned for today

1278
00:57:27,790 --> 00:57:33,887
is a chapter on graph nets, but if you continue reading forward

1279
00:57:33,887 --> 00:57:35,470
from the part that we assigned, you'll

1280
00:57:35,470 --> 00:57:38,210
see that there's a lot-- a lot more in that document.

1281
00:57:38,210 --> 00:57:39,910
It's a very good--

1282
00:57:39,910 --> 00:57:41,310
almost a book, really--

1283
00:57:41,310 --> 00:57:44,080
manuscript.

1284
00:57:44,080 --> 00:57:46,980
So in the last section of this lecture,

1285
00:57:46,980 --> 00:57:49,840
I want to talk a little bit about the theoretical properties

1286
00:57:49,840 --> 00:57:52,560
of graph nets, what types of--

1287
00:57:52,560 --> 00:57:54,227
what types of problems can they solve,

1288
00:57:54,227 --> 00:57:56,060
and what types of problems can't they solve,

1289
00:57:56,060 --> 00:57:58,920
and it's going to be related to approximation theory.

1290
00:57:58,920 --> 00:58:01,720
So to set this up, I'm going to give one more perspective on how

1291
00:58:01,720 --> 00:58:05,000
graph nets are operating, and this is the tree perspective

1292
00:58:05,000 --> 00:58:06,920
of what a node will see.

1293
00:58:06,920 --> 00:58:09,800
So here, we have a node of interest, node A,

1294
00:58:09,800 --> 00:58:14,280
it has neighbors B, C, and D. And

1295
00:58:14,280 --> 00:58:16,800
the message-passing algorithm, layer by layer,

1296
00:58:16,800 --> 00:58:17,793
will do the following.

1297
00:58:17,793 --> 00:58:19,460
This gray box is the aggregate function.

1298
00:58:19,460 --> 00:58:21,865
It's going to take messages from all the neighbors,

1299
00:58:21,865 --> 00:58:24,240
combine them, aggregate them together, and send a message

1300
00:58:24,240 --> 00:58:28,480
to node A. And so node A only is learning something

1301
00:58:28,480 --> 00:58:30,320
from these neighbors.

1302
00:58:30,320 --> 00:58:33,440
But if we run multiple steps of message passing

1303
00:58:33,440 --> 00:58:37,880
or we unroll this graph net deep--

1304
00:58:37,880 --> 00:58:39,960
so two steps, so two-layer graph net,

1305
00:58:39,960 --> 00:58:44,750
essentially-- now we'll get messages not only from B, C, D,

1306
00:58:44,750 --> 00:58:50,160
but also from A and C, going into B, and so forth.

1307
00:58:50,160 --> 00:58:53,440
So the information flow from the perspective--

1308
00:58:53,440 --> 00:58:54,940
suppose you're standing on the graph

1309
00:58:54,940 --> 00:58:57,640
at node A. This is who's talking to you,

1310
00:58:57,640 --> 00:59:00,340
and this is the funnel of information coming into you.

1311
00:59:00,340 --> 00:59:02,540
And every iteration of the message passing

1312
00:59:02,540 --> 00:59:05,360
will increase the depth of this tree by 1.

1313
00:59:10,460 --> 00:59:12,460
So the layer 2 representation of A

1314
00:59:12,460 --> 00:59:14,740
is going to be messages passed from the layer 1

1315
00:59:14,740 --> 00:59:16,740
representation of these other nodes, which

1316
00:59:16,740 --> 00:59:20,900
is going to be messages passed from the input, raw layer 0

1317
00:59:20,900 --> 00:59:24,140
node vectors for those nodes.

1318
00:59:24,140 --> 00:59:25,060
Question.

