01:09:24,660 --> 01:09:26,660
Structurally, these are different graphs, right?

1551
01:09:26,660 --> 01:09:29,319
One has these two closed planar regions,

1552
01:09:29,319 --> 01:09:33,180
and the other has two regions, but there are triangles.

1553
01:09:33,180 --> 01:09:36,080
These are structurally distinct graphs.

1554
01:09:38,620 --> 01:09:41,779
But let's look at the tree structure

1555
01:09:41,779 --> 01:09:44,899
of the top left red node.

1556
01:09:44,899 --> 01:09:47,740
It has exactly the same tree structure for both graphs.

1557
01:09:47,740 --> 01:09:49,800
We can expand those trees going further down,

1558
01:09:49,800 --> 01:09:52,899
but this is enough to make the point.

1559
01:09:52,899 --> 01:09:55,580
So the red node has a yellow node and a red node

1560
01:09:55,580 --> 01:09:57,620
as a neighbor, and the yellow node

1561
01:09:57,620 --> 01:09:59,998
has a red, yellow, and red node as neighbor.

1562
01:09:59,998 --> 01:10:02,040
And then we can just keep repeating that process.

1563
01:10:02,040 --> 01:10:04,620
You'll see they're entirely equivalent.

1564
01:10:04,620 --> 01:10:06,143
And yet, they're not the same graph.

1565
01:10:06,143 --> 01:10:08,060
So two graphs can have the same tree structure

1566
01:10:08,060 --> 01:10:09,700
but not be the same graph.

1567
01:10:09,700 --> 01:10:12,180
That means that if one of these molecules is toxic

1568
01:10:12,180 --> 01:10:13,763
and the other is not-- let's say these

1569
01:10:13,763 --> 01:10:16,280
are molecules and one of them is toxic and the other's not.

1570
01:10:16,280 --> 01:10:18,420
These are different physical things, and so one of them

1571
01:10:18,420 --> 01:10:19,120
could be different.

1572
01:10:19,120 --> 01:10:20,953
It could be toxic, and then it might not be.

1573
01:10:20,953 --> 01:10:23,520
A graph net will have to assign the same label to them.

1574
01:10:23,520 --> 01:10:24,020
Yeah.

1575
01:10:24,020 --> 01:10:28,080
AUDIENCE: So if you just gave each node in the graph

1576
01:10:28,080 --> 01:10:32,000
the one-hot encoding in addition to its node attribute,

1577
01:10:32,000 --> 01:10:32,897
then you could.

1578
01:10:32,897 --> 01:10:35,480
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so you're giving the answer to a question

1579
01:10:35,480 --> 01:10:37,120
I was going to ask in a few slides.

1580
01:10:37,120 --> 01:10:40,020
So if you simply give each node a one-hot encoding,

1581
01:10:40,020 --> 01:10:43,600
like, tell it its position in the graph,

1582
01:10:43,600 --> 01:10:45,655
then the symmetry can be broken.

1583
01:10:45,655 --> 01:10:47,280
But the power of graph nets is that you

1584
01:10:47,280 --> 01:10:50,540
want to be invariant to the ordering of the nodes.

1585
01:10:50,540 --> 01:10:52,160
And so by breaking that symmetry,

1586
01:10:52,160 --> 01:10:53,740
you break the permutation invariance.

1587
01:10:53,740 --> 01:10:57,680
So it's a trade-off.

1588
01:10:57,680 --> 01:10:59,700
Here's another case, a bit harder to see,

1589
01:10:59,700 --> 01:11:04,160
but these two graphs are equivalent according to a GNN.

1590
01:11:04,160 --> 01:11:07,680
But they're not the same graph.

1591
01:11:07,680 --> 01:11:09,520
So those are equivalence classes according

1592
01:11:09,520 --> 01:11:13,240
to the GNNs because a GNN can only

1593
01:11:13,240 --> 01:11:16,720
distinguish two graphs that have different neighborhood tree

1594
01:11:16,720 --> 01:11:17,920
structures.

1595
01:11:17,920 --> 01:11:20,720
Now, this actually connects to some deep and old theory

1596
01:11:20,720 --> 01:11:23,280
in graphs, graph isomorphism, where

1597
01:11:23,280 --> 01:11:26,330
you're trying to say, how can I tell if one graph is

1598
01:11:26,330 --> 01:11:28,090
the same as another graph?

1599
01:11:28,090 --> 01:11:30,690
And GNNs have the same power to distinguish graphs

1600
01:11:30,690 --> 01:11:35,370
as this thing called the one WL algorithm?

1601
01:11:35,370 --> 01:11:37,722
So some of you might have seen this before.

1602
01:11:37,722 --> 01:11:39,430
I'm not going to go into all the details.

1603
01:11:39,430 --> 01:11:41,750
But this is going back to the 1960s.

1604
01:11:41,750 --> 01:11:44,650
This is really classical graph theory.

1605
01:11:44,650 --> 01:11:47,308
And the Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm

1606
01:11:47,308 --> 01:11:48,850
is an algorithm that takes two graphs

1607
01:11:48,850 --> 01:11:51,130
and decides if they're the same or different.

1608
01:11:51,130 --> 01:11:54,003
And the one Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm

1609
01:11:54,003 --> 01:11:55,670
can distinguish certain kinds of graphs,

1610
01:11:55,670 --> 01:11:57,170
and it's the same kinds of graphs

1611
01:11:57,170 --> 01:11:58,710
that a GNN can distinguish.

1612
01:11:58,710 --> 01:12:01,770
And so whatever the Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm

1613
01:12:01,770 --> 01:12:03,750
can discriminate a GNN can discriminate.

1614
01:12:03,750 --> 01:12:08,050
And this is just a connection to some graph theory.

1615
01:12:08,050 --> 01:12:12,210
So roughly, the way that the Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm works

1616
01:12:12,210 --> 01:12:14,830
is you assign colors to the nodes in the graph,

1617
01:12:14,830 --> 01:12:17,010
and then you do some kind of propagation,

1618
01:12:17,010 --> 01:12:19,170
and you'd decide if two graphs are

1619
01:12:19,170 --> 01:12:25,270
the same if they result in the same coloring of the nodes.

1620
01:12:25,270 --> 01:12:30,270
And let's see.

1621
01:12:30,270 --> 01:12:34,270
So yeah, a GNN can, at best, distinguish the same graphs

1622
01:12:34,270 --> 01:12:36,830
that the one-dimensional Weisfeiler-Leman algorithm

1623
01:12:36,830 --> 01:12:37,950
can distinguish.

1624
01:12:37,950 --> 01:12:41,870
But another interesting thing is that it's not only, can best.

1625
01:12:41,870 --> 01:12:43,530
It's actually, you can achieve this.

1626
01:12:43,530 --> 01:12:48,750
So for any size graph, there exists a GNN such

1627
01:12:48,750 --> 01:12:52,710
that you can distinguish two graphs that

1628
01:12:52,710 --> 01:12:55,830
are distinguishable by the WL algorithm.

1629
01:12:55,830 --> 01:12:58,510
So we have universal approximation

1630
01:12:58,510 --> 01:13:02,710
within this family-- a universal ability

1631
01:13:02,710 --> 01:13:05,670
to discriminate within this family of graphs

1632
01:13:05,670 --> 01:13:09,030
that can be discriminated by this classical theory.

1633
01:13:09,030 --> 01:13:14,710
And just giving you the gist of why you get universality

1634
01:13:14,710 --> 01:13:16,910
within that family is--

1635
01:13:16,910 --> 01:13:17,970
it's a two-part argument.

1636
01:13:17,970 --> 01:13:21,770
The first part is that a arbitrary, nonlinear,

1637
01:13:21,770 --> 01:13:25,930
some nonlinear function is a universal approximator

1638
01:13:25,930 --> 01:13:28,970
of any multiset function, and MLPs

1639
01:13:28,970 --> 01:13:32,490
are universal approximators to arbitrary nonlinear functions.

1640
01:13:32,490 --> 01:13:36,090
So MLP-- some MLP is a universal approximator

1641
01:13:36,090 --> 01:13:40,705
to multiset functions.

1642
01:13:40,705 --> 01:13:43,010
And then, yeah, you can essentially

1643
01:13:43,010 --> 01:13:49,352
show that the aggregate in this form that

1644
01:13:49,352 --> 01:13:50,810
can take on a multiset function can

1645
01:13:50,810 --> 01:13:52,850
distinguish exactly the same class of algorithms

1646
01:13:52,850 --> 01:13:54,790
as the WL algorithm.

1647
01:13:54,790 --> 01:13:56,170
Yeah.

1648
01:13:56,170 --> 01:13:58,610
AUDIENCE: How common is it?

1649
01:13:58,610 --> 01:14:00,110
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, great question.

1650
01:14:00,110 --> 01:14:02,370
So how common is it to find two graphs that cannot be

1651
01:14:02,370 --> 01:14:04,570
distinguished in this way?

1652
01:14:04,570 --> 01:14:07,790
I think that last year, I said, oh, it's an edge case.

1653
01:14:07,790 --> 01:14:08,930
It's not too common.

1654
01:14:08,930 --> 01:14:11,450
And then I realized I don't actually know.

1655
01:14:11,450 --> 01:14:16,030
So it's hard to come up with them on pencil and paper.

1656
01:14:20,650 --> 01:14:23,093
My guess is that this is a theoretically important thing.

1657
01:14:23,093 --> 01:14:25,010
It's like an adversarial-- a worst-case thing.

1658
01:14:25,010 --> 01:14:28,880
Like, this could exist, and then all your machinery breaks.

1659
01:14:28,880 --> 01:14:30,630
But it would be really interesting to know

1660
01:14:30,630 --> 01:14:32,430
if actual molecules that you're trying

1661
01:14:32,430 --> 01:14:36,770
to classify the toxicity of would ever have this issue.

1662
01:14:36,770 --> 01:14:40,630
Would there ever be like a benign and a toxic molecule that

1663
01:14:40,630 --> 01:14:42,770
would be indistinguishable, according to graph net,

1664
01:14:42,770 --> 01:14:45,710
but actually have different effects on the body?

1665
01:14:45,710 --> 01:14:47,110
And the answer is, I don't know.

1666
01:14:47,110 --> 01:14:50,310
But I imagine that for health and safety-critical

1667
01:14:50,310 --> 01:14:54,190
applications, this is important to understand.

1668
01:14:54,190 --> 01:14:55,210
So this is a worst-case.

1669
01:14:55,210 --> 01:14:57,950
I'm not sure how common it is in practice.

1670
01:14:57,950 --> 01:14:59,550
Yeah.

1671
01:14:59,550 --> 01:15:00,050
OK.

1672
01:15:03,190 --> 01:15:06,030
So I do want to bring this back to empirical practice

1673
01:15:06,030 --> 01:15:09,370
to show that this really does make an important difference.

1674
01:15:09,370 --> 01:15:12,950
If you understand the theory of what

1675
01:15:12,950 --> 01:15:15,678
are the appropriate aggregation functions that

1676
01:15:15,678 --> 01:15:17,470
will lead you to be able to approximate all

1677
01:15:17,470 --> 01:15:19,910
the functions you care about within the set

1678
01:15:19,910 --> 01:15:23,680
of multiset functions, well, is that theory actually

1679
01:15:23,680 --> 01:15:25,060
important in practice?

1680
01:15:25,060 --> 01:15:27,400
So let's look at this example.

1681
01:15:27,400 --> 01:15:31,700
So here's a paper where we have training accuracy on the y-axis,

1682
01:15:31,700 --> 01:15:34,640
and we have the epochs of learning on the x-axis.

1683
01:15:34,640 --> 01:15:36,420
And higher is better.

1684
01:15:36,420 --> 01:15:42,220
So the red line is using an aggregation function,

1685
01:15:42,220 --> 01:15:44,560
which is MLP, some MLP.

1686
01:15:44,560 --> 01:15:47,960
So the Gs are an MLP for the red line.

1687
01:15:47,960 --> 01:15:51,320
And I stated that that is a universal approximator

1688
01:15:51,320 --> 01:15:53,320
to any multiset function.

1689
01:15:53,320 --> 01:15:57,012
And indeed, this is able to get 100% accuracy

1690
01:15:57,012 --> 01:15:58,220
on fitting the training data.

1691
01:15:58,220 --> 01:15:59,500
This is just the training accuracy,

1692
01:15:59,500 --> 01:16:00,840
so this is just about approximation, not

1693
01:16:00,840 --> 01:16:01,820
about generalization.

1694
01:16:01,820 --> 01:16:03,880
Can I fit the training data?

1695
01:16:03,880 --> 01:16:05,480
But the interesting thing is that if I

1696
01:16:05,480 --> 01:16:07,870
use a different aggregation operation--

1697
01:16:07,870 --> 01:16:09,920
like, you might be just hacking away in PyTorch

1698
01:16:09,920 --> 01:16:14,260
and think, oh, I'm not going to do MLP-- sum, MLP.

1699
01:16:14,260 --> 01:16:17,680
I'll just do sum and linear ReLU.

1700
01:16:17,680 --> 01:16:19,220
So linear ReLU is not a full MLP.

1701
01:16:19,220 --> 01:16:20,860
It's not a universal approximator.

1702
01:16:20,860 --> 01:16:22,860
We have to have linear ReLU linear

1703
01:16:22,860 --> 01:16:25,333
is a universal approximator, but linear ReLU is not.

1704
01:16:25,333 --> 01:16:28,000
So let's say I just decided, I'm going to save some computation.

1705
01:16:28,000 --> 01:16:30,180
I'm going to skip that line in PyTorch.

1706
01:16:30,180 --> 01:16:32,682
Well, that's not a universal approximator.

1707
01:16:32,682 --> 01:16:35,140
And for this type of data, this protein prediction problem,

1708
01:16:35,140 --> 01:16:36,557
I don't know the domain very well.

1709
01:16:36,557 --> 01:16:38,100
But for this problem, you actually

1710
01:16:38,100 --> 01:16:40,920
just plateau at a lower level of accuracy.

1711
01:16:40,920 --> 01:16:44,422
And I suppose that's because you can't even approximate the data

1712
01:16:44,422 --> 01:16:46,380
you're trying to approximate because you're not

1713
01:16:46,380 --> 01:16:49,780
using an approximation function that has that capacity.

1714
01:16:49,780 --> 01:16:51,860
So the theory actually is meaningful here.

1715
01:16:51,860 --> 01:16:55,380
And sometimes I think that it's hard to see, like,

1716
01:16:55,380 --> 01:16:58,503
why do we need to learn all this theory of what kinds

1717
01:16:58,503 --> 01:17:00,420
of functions can be approximated by what kinds

1718
01:17:00,420 --> 01:17:01,580
of neural networks?

1719
01:17:01,580 --> 01:17:04,000
Well, it actually can matter in practice.

1720
01:17:04,000 --> 01:17:06,460
And if you use something that does not have the capacity

1721
01:17:06,460 --> 01:17:08,420
to approximate the family you care about,

1722
01:17:08,420 --> 01:17:10,940
you can just be fundamentally limited.

1723
01:17:10,940 --> 01:17:14,978
The other curves are even worse aggregation functions.

1724
01:17:14,978 --> 01:17:16,520
So here's one really interesting one.

1725
01:17:16,520 --> 01:17:17,280
Look at this one.

1726
01:17:17,280 --> 01:17:19,280
That is, I just used-- the blue curve is using

1727
01:17:19,280 --> 01:17:21,560
the mean as opposed to the sum.

1728
01:17:21,560 --> 01:17:25,180
So I believe it's the same as the equation at the top.

1729
01:17:25,180 --> 01:17:28,280
But rather than taking a sum, I take the mean.

1730
01:17:28,280 --> 01:17:29,540
And suddenly, it doesn't work.

1731
01:17:29,540 --> 01:17:33,040
And you might think, that seems like a tiny, tiny difference.

1732
01:17:33,040 --> 01:17:35,740
Instead of taking the sum over my node, my neighbors,

1733
01:17:35,740 --> 01:17:37,520
I'm taking the mean over my neighbors.

1734
01:17:37,520 --> 01:17:38,742
Surely that should work.

1735
01:17:38,742 --> 01:17:40,700
And you might be coding this way and just said,

1736
01:17:40,700 --> 01:17:43,155
oh, I bet taking the average as opposed to the sum

1737
01:17:43,155 --> 01:17:44,780
is going to be more numerically stable.

1738
01:17:44,780 --> 01:17:45,740
I'll just do that.

1739
01:17:45,740 --> 01:17:47,660
Well, the thing would fail completely.

1740
01:17:47,660 --> 01:17:52,920
Anyone know why taking the mean is going to lose information

1741
01:17:52,920 --> 01:17:54,200
that the sum has?

1742
01:17:54,200 --> 01:17:57,000
Let's see if we can go-- right back here.

1743
01:17:57,000 --> 01:17:57,702
Yeah.

1744
01:17:57,702 --> 01:17:59,160
AUDIENCE: Maybe you can't calculate

1745
01:17:59,160 --> 01:18:00,960
how many neighbors [INAUDIBLE].

1746
01:18:00,960 --> 01:18:01,560
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so maybe you

1747
01:18:01,560 --> 01:18:03,140
can't calculate how many neighbors.

1748
01:18:03,140 --> 01:18:05,660
So taking the sum knows something

1749
01:18:05,660 --> 01:18:07,160
about the total number of neighbors,

1750
01:18:07,160 --> 01:18:09,040
because it gets bigger with more neighbors.

1751
01:18:09,040 --> 01:18:11,580
Taking the mean divides by the number of neighbors,

1752
01:18:11,580 --> 01:18:13,140
so it loses information.

1753
01:18:13,140 --> 01:18:15,440
And by losing that information on this problem,

1754
01:18:15,440 --> 01:18:17,700
you just get-- you can't actually

1755
01:18:17,700 --> 01:18:20,020
learn the true function because you required knowing

1756
01:18:20,020 --> 01:18:21,533
the number of neighbors.

1757
01:18:21,533 --> 01:18:24,200
So these tiny little details can actually make a big difference,

1758
01:18:24,200 --> 01:18:26,860
and it's important to know that.

1759
01:18:26,860 --> 01:18:32,540
So here's a few other properties of graphs that cannot be learned

1760
01:18:32,540 --> 01:18:35,740
with a conventional GNN as I've described it so far.

1761
01:18:35,740 --> 01:18:39,640
So you can't estimate the longest cycle in a graph.

1762
01:18:39,640 --> 01:18:42,040
You can't estimate the diameter of the graph.

1763
01:18:42,040 --> 01:18:44,332
These are things that, no matter how much training data

1764
01:18:44,332 --> 01:18:46,130
you have, the graph net is--

1765
01:18:46,130 --> 01:18:48,380
there's going to be cases where you'll get arbitrarily

1766
01:18:48,380 --> 01:18:53,020
large error on these estimates because you can construct

1767
01:18:53,020 --> 01:18:55,597
two graphs that are isomorphic according to the GNN, two

1768
01:18:55,597 --> 01:18:57,180
graphs that have the same neighborhood

1769
01:18:57,180 --> 01:18:59,620
structure but different diameters.

1770
01:18:59,620 --> 01:19:02,200
OK, so there's fundamental limits to what GNNs can do.

1771
01:19:02,200 --> 01:19:04,433
But how do we get around these fundamental limits?

1772
01:19:04,433 --> 01:19:05,600
We heard the answer already.

1773
01:19:05,600 --> 01:19:08,320
But anyone want to shout it out again?

1774
01:19:08,320 --> 01:19:11,040
What would be a way of making a graph

1775
01:19:11,040 --> 01:19:18,390
net that is not going to be invariant to permutations

1776
01:19:18,390 --> 01:19:20,210
of the nodes?

1777
01:19:20,210 --> 01:19:21,252
AUDIENCE: One-hot coding?

1778
01:19:21,252 --> 01:19:22,585
PHILLIP ISOLA: One-hot encoding.

1779
01:19:22,585 --> 01:19:23,270
Yeah.

1780
01:19:23,270 --> 01:19:26,740
So more generally, positional encodings.

1781
01:19:26,740 --> 01:19:28,990
So just like we can make a convolutional network which

1782
01:19:28,990 --> 01:19:30,550
is not translation-equivariant, we

1783
01:19:30,550 --> 01:19:33,430
can make a graph net that is not invariant to permutations

1784
01:19:33,430 --> 01:19:35,830
by adding-- telling each node where it is,

1785
01:19:35,830 --> 01:19:37,670
what position it's at.

1786
01:19:37,670 --> 01:19:40,230
And this allows you to break some of these symmetries

1787
01:19:40,230 --> 01:19:44,230
with these equivalence classes of two graphs that

1788
01:19:44,230 --> 01:19:46,470
have the same neighborhood structure because now

1789
01:19:46,470 --> 01:19:50,190
we actually can see that the nodes have different positions

1790
01:19:50,190 --> 01:19:51,670
in the graph.

1791
01:19:51,670 --> 01:19:53,930
And one of the common ways of doing this--

1792
01:19:53,930 --> 01:19:58,510
remember that Sarah talked about adding a sinusoidal positional

1793
01:19:58,510 --> 01:20:03,150
encoding, where every position in your image is going to get--

1794
01:20:03,150 --> 01:20:05,930
it's going to get a vector that tells us where it is.

1795
01:20:05,930 --> 01:20:06,670
Is it upper left?

1796
01:20:06,670 --> 01:20:07,290
Is it middle?

1797
01:20:07,290 --> 01:20:08,230
Is it right?

1798
01:20:08,230 --> 01:20:10,750
Well, in a graph, we can get a kind of generalized version

1799
01:20:10,750 --> 01:20:13,630
of where you are in the graph using the eigenvectors

1800
01:20:13,630 --> 01:20:14,793
of the graph Laplacian.

1801
01:20:14,793 --> 01:20:16,710
So I'm not going to talk in detail about that.

1802
01:20:16,710 --> 01:20:18,710
But this is just a generalized coordinate system

1803
01:20:18,710 --> 01:20:21,090
for your location of a node within a graph.

1804
01:20:21,090 --> 01:20:24,170
And if you add those to your graph node

1805
01:20:24,170 --> 01:20:27,550
inputs, the node vectors of the input layer to the graph net,

1806
01:20:27,550 --> 01:20:29,750
then you will break some of these symmetries.

1807
01:20:29,750 --> 01:20:32,330
And that can be good or bad because now you might not

1808
01:20:32,330 --> 01:20:34,210
generalize to new permutations, but you

1809
01:20:34,210 --> 01:20:36,752
will be able to discriminate things you couldn't discriminate

1810
01:20:36,752 --> 01:20:37,410
before.

1811
01:20:37,410 --> 01:20:40,130
So it's a trade-off.

1812
01:20:40,130 --> 01:20:44,350
So yeah, that's just like positional encoding in CNNs,

1813
01:20:44,350 --> 01:20:46,090
which we saw on the previous day.

1814
01:20:46,090 --> 01:20:48,602
So I'm going to end there.

1815
01:20:48,602 --> 01:20:50,810
We saw that graph nets are appropriate for processing

1816
01:20:50,810 --> 01:20:52,088
graph-structured data.

1817
01:20:52,088 --> 01:20:54,130
It turns out that a special case of the graph net

1818
01:20:54,130 --> 01:20:55,450
is called the transformer, and that's

1819
01:20:55,450 --> 01:20:57,200
just the dominant architecture these days.

1820
01:20:57,200 --> 01:21:00,250
And we're going to hear a lot more about that in a week or so.

1821
01:21:00,250 --> 01:21:02,390
And I can take questions up here at the end,

1822
01:21:02,390 --> 01:21:03,910
but you're welcome to all leave now.

1823
01:21:03,910 --> 01:21:05,640
Thank you.
